#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172085 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171891 :question/id #uuid "bccee2b9-f937-5df5-b3cf-4e45096c9ac3" :question/math-academy-id "e-9465" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on $\\mathbb R^2?$\n\n- $\\mathbf A(x,y) = \\sqrt{x}\\,\\mathbf i + y^3\\,\\mathbf j$\n\n- $\\mathbf B(x,y,z) = \\ln y\\,\\mathbf i - (x+y)\\,\\mathbf j  + z\\,\\mathbf k$\n\n- $\\mathbf C(t) = \\sin{t}\\,\\mathbf i + (2-t^3)\\,\\mathbf j$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^2.$ A vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ is a function $\\mathbf F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $\\mathbf F(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $\\mathbf A(x,y)$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2.$ It maps every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\:|\\: x \\geq 0\\big\\}\n$$\n\nto a vector in $\\mathbb R^2.$\n\n- The function $\\mathbf B(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2.$ It is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$\n\n- The function $\\mathbf C(t)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2.$  It assigns points in $\\mathbb{R}$ (not $\\mathbb{R^2}$) to vectors in $\\mathbb{R^2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "019e5a17-1aab-565b-8881-447c86a9a7ec" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172278 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15deb0f8-c55d-57a2-81f6-2142722bdf77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253860} {:answer/id #uuid "178907e3-8b45-599f-b8d0-3349646c4f94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253873} {:answer/id #uuid "38438f74-0aa6-57ff-b661-78f5f62ca8ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254157} {:answer/id #uuid "4f2f9fa9-ddf1-5a6b-92cb-311164a1df91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254357} {:answer/id #uuid "f4597c3d-7459-57b3-a142-69729b5c48f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255809}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38438f74-0aa6-57ff-b661-78f5f62ca8ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254157} :answer-field/id #uuid "4824be83-0ec4-51e9-874c-98cf1bb977d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256060}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b2d1a20d-340e-5980-ad18-92b97e0270ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-122465" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $G(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i-{y}^{2}\\,j$\n- $H(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,i-{e}^{xy}\\,j$\n- $K(x,y,z)=x\\,i-y\\,j+z\\,k$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{2}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{2}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $F(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $G(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ to a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $H(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,y>0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $K(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172283 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5580f132-72e3-58fb-813f-49af468ded0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254416} {:answer/id #uuid "5db5413c-3f34-5816-961d-b448ce