id #edb/ref 17592186256685 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7dcdb238-84f5-50d1-81fb-d46208d9425f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254783} {:answer/id #uuid "94b9519b-6e8f-5cb8-8040-62148a141e85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254977} {:answer/id #uuid "a70688af-7165-571e-8ce0-430e79655143" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255143} {:answer/id #uuid "b62432e8-8719-5cba-8d52-19c52dce26b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255281} {:answer/id #uuid "f2f16d7b-50db-57c4-829a-2c1c1353919f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255794}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a70688af-7165-571e-8ce0-430e79655143" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255143} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c523d76-2d1e-5e8c-9b53-1b2e99146620" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255934}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "82c19ae8-c5db-5680-ac18-77dd108b7959" :question/math-academy-id "q-122650" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $U(x,y)=⟨\\frac{1}{x+2},\\sqrt{x-y}⟩$\n- $V(x,y)=⟨x{e}^{y},1-y⟩$\n- $W(x,y,z)=⟨x,-y,-z⟩$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{2}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{2}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $F(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $U(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x\\ne -2,\\,x-y\\ge 0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $V(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ to a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $W(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256687 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c408810-efc6-5638-a618-d28745a9b48a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253767} {:answer/id #uuid "4849dc08-25ab-5a55-b530-c3ded2927c4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254287} {:answer/id #uuid "a6b8d887-618e-5d51-b874-9c8b99a43860" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255139} {:answer/id #uuid "a90bb982-3a8e-52d4-919e-59317069be3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255159} {:answer/id #uuid "b7e3eaf3-da05-5aa5-b7ef-f5fd16504087" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255303}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4849dc08-25ab-5a55-b530-c3ded2927c4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254287} :answer-field/id #uuid "321403c5-6d4b-5e0e-88e6-f677e7259264" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256009}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "212db81f-3e39-5c00-88aa-953a8a56ffb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-122653" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $A(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y),\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)⟩$\n- $B(x,y,z)=⟨-2x,3{y}^{3},{z}^{5}⟩$\n- $C(t)=⟨2-{t}^{2},{e}^{t-1}⟩$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{2}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{2}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $F(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $A(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x+y>0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $B(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $C(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$  It assigns points in $R$ (not ${R}^{2}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256693 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49a2f5e2-8b9e-5771-8e4c-130ad318d884" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254303} {:answer/id #uuid "740aab86-ef29-5671-b4b8-e9ccd3a8d22e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254696} {:answer/id #uuid "832c1dab-f56a-57a4-bb5b-347d7aa89125" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254836} {:answer/id #uuid "d057ce44-c181-5fc1-a138-ce9005660990" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255525} {:answer/id #uuid "fd43e487-e4ea-56ad-b5c6-843be58a5b74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255896}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d057ce44-c181-5fc1-a138-ce9005660990" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255525} :answer-field/id #uuid "b10ade94-378e-5853-8e3a-658322f792bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256242}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4f63ae22-12f3-552d-bede-acf1007c6564" :question/math-academy-id "q-122678" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $U(x,y)=⟨(x+2){e}^{y},\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x-y}⟩$\n- $V(x,y,z)=⟨5,{x}^{3}y,1-{y}^{3}⟩$\n- $W(x,y)=⟨x-3{y}^{2},2-y⟩$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{2}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{2}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $F(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $U(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x\\ne y}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $K(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $V(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ to a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380657 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34c119eb-a2e9-5e7b-aece-194690e3575f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380659} {:answer/id #uuid "f11bcfb0-554a-553b-a2cd-9d076207bb59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380660} {:answer/id #uuid "7d17c850-0945-5f89-a53a-ffaee5e1a53e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380661} {:answer/id #uuid "04ec628e-d678-57c6-afb0-df8af8ce90b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380662} {:answer/id #uuid "649bb3e8-6868-569d-a178-c5ab91222bdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380663}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f11bcfb0-554a-553b-a2cd-9d076207bb59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380660} :answer-field/id #uuid "caef7be4-fa1f-5ecd-8432-0db8e2abf1fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380658}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1de1af2b-64e8-50cf-80c9-f414743fe7ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-122641" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $G(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j$\n- $H(t)=\\sqrt{t}\\,i+t{e}^{t}\\,j$\n- $K(x,y,z)=2x\\,i+y\\,j-3z\\,k$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{2}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{2}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $F(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $G(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ to a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $H(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$  It assigns points in $R$ (not ${R}^{2}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $K(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380664 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16526cbf-eed9-5dc8-b49d-07c80cc53aee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380666} {:answer/id #uuid "76cc8d11-9c5e-5b66-857e-79223281043f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380667} {:answer/id #uuid "1bfe71e4-e268-5307-ad87-4f7d22a6a41f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380668} {:answer/id #uuid "85e5e6ca-2257-5462-aa74-7664e109c0ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380669} {:answer/id #uuid "d3ffeac8-d5ff-53ee-ad33-6d914cd947c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380670}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3ffeac8-d5ff-53ee-ad33-6d914cd947c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380670} :answer-field/id #uuid "21763b78-6aa3-55ed-be0a-c14268a02c40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380665}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "502cb71c-02d6-5e14-a31b-2cee28a0a5ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-122656" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $M(x,y)=⟨y-{x}^{2},3x+{y}^{2},\\frac{1}{x+y}⟩$\n- $N(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+1,1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x⟩$\n- $P(t)=⟨{t}^{3},\\sqrt{1-t}\\,⟩$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{2}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{2}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $F(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $M(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It assigns points in  ${R}^{2}$ to vectors in ${R}^{3}$ (not in ${R}^{2}$).\n\n- The function $N(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ to a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $P(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It assigns points in  $R$ \n(not ${R}^{2}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380678 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d818659d-904f-56ab-a894-ba5c08da4a69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380680} {:answer/id #uuid "073a4146-a75e-5400-a120-20b8b4dcbe03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380681} {:answer/id #uuid "25a00a6a-d7e9-5e03-949c-8562fa76683a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380682} {:answer/id #uuid "60870366-832c-58f3-ab71-17ad52ae15cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380683} {:answer/id #uuid "4a72a1c4-87b7-54cc-8c12-34d1ba68b017" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60870366-832c-58f3-ab71-17ad52ae15cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380683} :answer-field/id #uuid "cee31fa4-3f78-5e6b-b4f0-0888305b3c27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380679}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5b352a16-fe6f-5946-bac9-7b334007947a" :question/math-academy-id "q-122675" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $C(x,y)=⟨\\frac{1}{x+y},{x}^{2}{e}^{y}⟩$\n- $D(x,y)=⟨{y}^{3}-3,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-y)⟩$\n- $E(x,y)=⟨x+1,y+2,x-y⟩$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{2}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{2}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $F(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $C(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x+y\\ne 0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $D(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ to a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $E(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ to vectors in ${R}^{3}$ (not ${R}^{2}$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields on Two-Dimensions"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172086 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171892 :question/id #uuid "193765c8-ea2c-5d90-b902-09d6d44a3a67" :question/math-academy-id "e-9466" :question/problem "Find the domain of the vector field $\\mathbf F$ on $\\mathbb R^2,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) =\\dfrac{1}{x^2+y^2}\\, \\mathbf{i} + \\sqrt{1+x-y} \\, \\mathbf{j}\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $\\mathbf F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) = P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i + Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf j,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y) =\\dfrac{1}{x^2+y^2}, \\qquad Q(x,y) = \\sqrt{1+x-y} .\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $\\mathbf F\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\nx^2 + y^2 = 0 \\quad\\Longrightarrow \\quad x=y = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nD_1 = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\: | \\: (x,y)\\neq (0,0)\\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe region $D_1$ is the entire plane $\\mathbb R^2$ with the origin removed, as shown below.\n\n![](images/d4/d4a418bc0dbfc61c0e55c494db51ee0fa8125b6b7056ccbe4e32ddca82574dc2.png)\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the radical is positive or zero. To find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n1+x - y \\geq 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad y\\leq 1 + x\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $Q$ is given by\n\n$$\nD_2 = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\: | \\: y \\leq 1 + x\\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe region $D_2,$ shown below:\n\n![](images/24/242903cc94103f8bb9cdc190a229fe7e86395f44600e8318d728fea3bc339385.png)\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $\\mathbf F$ is given by \n\n$$\nD =  D_1\\cap D_2 = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\: | \\: (x,y)\\neq 0,\\, y \\leq 1 + x\\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/f0/f02ca21443a81cdb039a3d97bab0b0f1036219bb89ecbdaa32386cbc4e5da6e3.png)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67b7ea90-38e0-54b5-b76e-816beb0d48f9" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172280 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "423fabcb-e2a7-5a29-9594-edebd6450c5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254235} {:answer/id #uuid "5f73033d-d5ff-5da8-8c1c-28671e834b1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254522} {:answer/id #uuid "74831d73-da33-5161-a77e-fdc419d47566" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,y\\ne \\pm x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254699} {:answer/id #uuid "8bd3c407-37a0-54c9-8259-7c347f371005" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,(x,y)\\ne 0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254904} {:answer/id #uuid "8dc43b23-9268-520e-aab5-397b548f35f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,y\\ne x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254920}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74831d73-da33-5161-a77e-fdc419d47566" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,y\\ne \\pm x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254699} :answer-field/id #uuid "7898097d-ceac-5c85-876e-b05ab6520f8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256142}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e58a710f-da20-5986-98eb-b29ea7b4485f" :question/math-academy-id "q-122474" :question/problem "Determine the domain of the vector field $F$ on ${R}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,j\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $F:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=y=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,(x,y)\\ne (0,0)}.\n$$\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\pm x\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $Q$ is given by\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,y\\ne \\pm x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,y\\ne \\pm x}\\,.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172281 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "129904d8-64df-5ea9-8154-f524933d7832" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e1/e1d9695fa4da169a7ec9c1f3ff20db320212bce34047fc13bb27028aadc95d79.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253832} {:answer/id #uuid "6f7205d3-5a29-5421-97fb-b1fb12155b6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/30/30462ed1f25749ac4ef05cf9ca9573508ae28a0799d7986dd005f71fdac15679.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254657} {:answer/id #uuid "a17c075f-c15e-5440-a087-1a116b3c38b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c6/c61552b7b9d8c24e438a8fa2d0a9248a3938c8e3512ca4a012d8b3342dd47a0f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255093} {:answer/id #uuid "a546db51-b877-5a6b-b55a-3fb457be5c19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/23/237dbec35d2ea6556014373efccbf1d34c31404fde3a015feb3c433056c63a3a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255128} {:answer/id #uuid "ff640f01-fdc3-54ce-a2f5-a80d67136507" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/60/606f516cb05ba0922cc63cd672a0df864bea121d9c50982a50d3c6f61465f3bf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255917}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f7205d3-5a29-5421-97fb-b1fb12155b6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/30/30462ed1f25749ac4ef05cf9ca9573508ae28a0799d7986dd005f71fdac15679.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254657} :answer-field/id #uuid "c53c4cc1-6e27-599f-b5f7-0fbae5ebf382" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256268}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "46b9e86b-e188-5e02-ada4-a07528592166" :question/math-academy-id "q-122518" :question/problem "Determine which of the following shaded regions depicts the domain of the vector field $F$ on ${R}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}},\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $F:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the radical is positive. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}>0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<9.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{1},$ which is the interior of a circle of radius $3,$ as shown below:\n\n![](images/e5/e54618f7e72d70f299686ca2846a1d6fa0caccb8daecb090b3a653271c88fcd3.png)\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=y=0.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{2},$ which is the entire plane ${R}^{2}$ with the origin removed:\n\n![](images/5b/5b77ad4e9574e94e6a05eee7680f4b57d0190d40583c37c2097acef958cab7e2.png)\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $F$ is given by ${D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2},$ shown below.\n\n![](images/30/30462ed1f25749ac4ef05cf9ca9573508ae28a0799d7986dd005f71fdac15679.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256688 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56102ac4-0111-50bf-baa1-120344804ec0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x<y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254425} {:answer/id #uuid "76a03f33-46e2-5d1c-8929-cfef138e411e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x\\ne y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254717} {:answer/id #uuid "c6fa9022-755b-5cfe-8215-5e56801bef47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,(x,y)\\ne (0,0),\\,x\\ge y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255447} {:answer/id #uuid "de3053b1-0e26-5159-83fc-ae08b0b64b4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,(x,y)\\ne (0,0)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255641} {:answer/id #uuid "eff20b85-4c4c-5b34-8add-3733f5aa6cba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x>y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255767}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eff20b85-4c4c-5b34-8add-3733f5aa6cba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x>y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255767} :answer-field/id #uuid "32eb6772-d2dc-5309-9bae-71a60e83b4a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256010}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9d82b252-32f5-5b9f-80ce-d3ae2209d9d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-122657" :question/problem "Determine the domain of the vector field $F$ on ${R}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)\\,i+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y),\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $F:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the logarithm is positive. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\nx-y>0\\,⟹\\,x>y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x>y}.\n$$\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=y=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $Q$ is given by\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,(x,y)\\ne (0,0)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x>y}\\,.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256691 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d0def04-0c36-58a0-a01f-2ed0f1bb2044" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4a/4a15607520f8ef05913aab5d1724c8b3158457e56d455b6eb756707263494308.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254070} {:answer/id #uuid "2d8a7024-c0df-5add-9db4-ef0e6d955c88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/26/26709ab7fa3cf1962ff9dca9be510cf5c24f3568f82347a8349e77dc5095fb4c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254077} {:answer/id #uuid "3285decd-3c25-54c5-a110-1f6bbe59e735" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a3/a3884061e6c5f8b5310a8b0bcf06ac844b12b418497d9f6e5b3beced6147d964.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254108} {:answer/id #uuid "5cacab87-de13-5e9a-892c-1c5b560e306c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0e/0e6843335d08bc42a95a5801aa683aeb2a043df0cd7edc03ffbc36a9385fee2a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254490} {:answer/id #uuid "f37057c0-c75a-55e4-bbb0-a9e433ba13c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/24/2469abf572b9b6fd9da28ae81951b99acdca9b97275af2bc0a96d3411d7fbea1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255803}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f37057c0-c75a-55e4-bbb0-a9e433ba13c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/24/2469abf572b9b6fd9da28ae81951b99acdca9b97275af2bc0a96d3411d7fbea1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255803} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc8fcbe1-118d-5975-9f5d-fda6bda82689" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256289}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "81ef4329-ef48-5a14-8457-2a79f1d3df80" :question/math-academy-id "q-122671" :question/problem "Determine which of the following shaded regions depicts the domain of the vector field $F$ on ${R}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}},\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $F:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the radical is positive or equal to zero. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{1},$ which is the interior of a circle of radius $1,$ as shown below:\n\n![](images/9f/9f5f11a4ca31cb796f5a95a1541aa946e24fd1b7079ab8069e5743d41d92d764.png)\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=y=0.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{2},$ which is the entire plane ${R}^{2}$ with the origin removed:\n\n![](images/5b/5b77ad4e9574e94e6a05eee7680f4b57d0190d40583c37c2097acef958cab7e2.png)\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $F$ is given by ${D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2},$ shown below.\n\n![](images/95/95e8d137f90135de1727451fbc96ddfcc325e9e4461054615c91743a4a9ea54a.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256704 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23be673f-249f-54d5-8bba-1196cc91218d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/11/11cfd599bbfe85c9f3c7a23d5b95c6bd2b3ff199c6c0a4777429123afc74cb8c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253992} {:answer/id #uuid "2d5e44b9-db64-5b67-b712-489ffc553051" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ff/ff0774689ae7215b624ea7eb3bed8f68a673fba4bcc4ceeb73f82b9081c32d4f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254074} {:answer/id #uuid "8cf757cd-3613-5b80-9f15-fd5bdceadfc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4a/4aeebc183f9a6676f7487fce53ec46ad0f2cc91a87847d8a230471dc638ea448.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254915} {:answer/id #uuid "da8daeb9-2bcb-50c1-b44c-600775cb8bb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/de/de222a9251e08861dd06fdee593cc10557f0be1d2c7d891395512d699b35aa63.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255609} {:answer/id #uuid "e605afc3-fcef-5e1e-a9be-583c83c6dfe1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/49/49000dd6905017bc64c9a67b6d8a2ea475a684477ac6e02d6ed15c4c7dbbb3aa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255698}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e605afc3-fcef-5e1e-a9be-583c83c6dfe1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/49/49000dd6905017bc64c9a67b6d8a2ea475a684477ac6e02d6ed15c4c7dbbb3aa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255698} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b83f961-43ac-5ecd-a230-9fdb901ae8cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255998}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "977dd2df-2a75-5c51-b25e-8edbacd60b15" :question/math-academy-id "q-122710" :question/problem "Determine which of the following shaded regions depicts the domain of the vector field $F$ on ${R}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-{x}^{2})\\,i+x\\sqrt{1-|y|}\\,j\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-{x}^{2}),\\,Q(x,y)=x\\sqrt{1-|y|}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $F:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the logarithm is positive. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n1-{x}^{2}>0\\,⟹\\,-1<x<1.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{1},$ shown below:\n\n![](images/89/89c78ec587826c15c90ef4ffdfc0fd9f8af3d7b4d3d1cc099ce24b883b9842c2.png)\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined the expression under the radical is positive or equal to zero. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n1-|y|\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,-1\\le y\\le 1.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{2},$ shown below:\n\n![](images/ad/addd649aa739711fd34ebd3e82579c8c0100a2b9a109d5cafabde750bc94eb9a.png)\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $F$ is given by ${D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2},$ shown below.\n\n![](images/49/49000dd6905017bc64c9a67b6d8a2ea475a684477ac6e02d6ed15c4c7dbbb3aa.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380671 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8cc3763-c596-539c-84ff-fc43a34ca838" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<4,\\,x\\ne 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380673} {:answer/id #uuid "e393beba-f44d-54af-a58f-f17495b74529" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380674} {:answer/id #uuid "d5a628af-d843-5691-850a-a01497b612f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 4,\\,x\\ne 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380675} {:answer/id #uuid "5ea6c947-7540-5f8e-9661-c097d1519263" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380676} {:answer/id #uuid "6105c7e2-8e73-526d-b477-10f7ed9a3aac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380677}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5a628af-d843-5691-850a-a01497b612f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 4,\\,x\\ne 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380675} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ced28f1-8592-507f-b2f3-ca4edef0e463" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380672}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "76ab55a5-b7ae-513b-bef2-54d7a5d78561" :question/math-academy-id "q-122660" :question/problem "Determine the domain of the vector field $F$ on ${R}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4})\\,i+\\frac{xy}{x-1}\\,j\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4}),\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{xy}{x-1}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $F:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4=0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 4}.\n$$\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\nx-1=0\\,⟹\\,x=1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $Q$ is given by\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x\\ne 1}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 4,\\,x\\ne 1}\\,.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380685 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ac4dc2b-7925-5bb6-a8d7-26be528ca51e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/59/59ec29347dea9437c8217a208bde0a8b200e37fcbce9c223d0358011a0685297.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380687} {:answer/id #uuid "de160c2f-72c8-5594-b423-821d641cf29b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/27/27f151bdd3d619de0988b786e6b7517a7f7586844f9a7a35bf651063e7a7dfd1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380688} {:answer/id #uuid "fc0e97a0-e2b2-5364-a798-7ba629c78a40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fa/fadd7ae69d155ebf23586b88369da8f6624b1919942dad543917592f2d3c195c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380689} {:answer/id #uuid "7de70ea1-561a-5260-a36d-8f1436c265b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/cb/cb10aa8f4d930ec7422da7dedfa0650244dceb01afe7cc849ed2bb16ffd3e44d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380690} {:answer/id #uuid "72aa0994-f928-5da5-8344-fae3471548d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a1/a1ee85bca08b21494e6f1f47de3abcbd71d2d01117795ef91d8ee3e038b36271.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380691}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de160c2f-72c8-5594-b423-821d641cf29b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/27/27f151bdd3d619de0988b786e6b7517a7f7586844f9a7a35bf651063e7a7dfd1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380688} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab0fa5e8-8931-50fe-a99e-b67ba6230eec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380686}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e932ad34-5aaf-594d-8943-da2547f2acb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-122677" :question/problem "Determine which of the following shaded regions depicts the domain of the vector field $F$ on ${R}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y-x}}\\,i+\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,j\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y-x}},\\,Q(x,y)=\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $F:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the radical is positive. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\ny-x>0\\,⟹\\,y>x.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{1},$ shown below:\n\n![](images/c7/c78759bc8f9926159065129e00c1fc8b4341ab83ad40cc87e67a2e82b3e6b089.png)\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the radical is positive or equal to zero. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n2-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 2.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{2},$ which is a circle of radius $\\sqrt{2},$ as shown below:\n\n![](images/bd/bdf02ff330f256327d0c9f1147c388b25184610b36ea7ab77bb2c570ec3be19a.png)\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $F$ is given by ${D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2},$ shown below.\n\n![](images/0d/0d3180a60e33c05e904c8fc00fdec4197510198fef3de3fa8c7b3549609205f6.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387222 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d59160e7-9169-5e83-a5c4-ad0518caff28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/21/2132ac0140fb0b96a876fd04a1069d6922662c72d4d8f4b8880eacae458f8181.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387224} {:answer/id #uuid "d5e66ef4-c389-503c-adac-bf6a7d3d4bc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fe/fefa684ba6cc6b2e8060c8b0860295177bade694786439317d6cee3d8cf51da8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387225} {:answer/id #uuid "1a97f507-5b03-5608-9bf1-7d0e5956f463" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/55/559ffda45a088810dcaa81628b8767bfedc64ca7f6b7ebd82aebdb2ed4a7ba00.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387226} {:answer/id #uuid "e78dff93-70e1-5457-996d-4e768112af8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/45/459b65c41ef9535d55703a64f3c9e4bfe9ffd9263a6a0f5dbf68405fb22c347d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387227} {:answer/id #uuid "0f013635-08f3-5ce8-b2d1-e6ffd2a329b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9a/9a20235e26590dbc6ddc5bdaaaa5dd484f5caf02e510bfabf2139ed7add5fa2d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387228}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a97f507-5b03-5608-9bf1-7d0e5956f463" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/55/559ffda45a088810dcaa81628b8767bfedc64ca7f6b7ebd82aebdb2ed4a7ba00.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387226} :answer-field/id #uuid "6702c2d4-7e00-59d1-8dcc-92a8c2e4e69d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387223}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "57d2166f-4843-548f-9e93-704a19813c47" :question/math-academy-id "q-122554" :question/problem "Determine which of the following shaded regions depicts the domain of the vector field $F$ on ${R}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{x-y}\\,j\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}},\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{x-y}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $F:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the radical is positive. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n3-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}>0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<3.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{1},$ which is the interior of a circle of radius $\\sqrt{3},$ as shown below:\n\n![](images/f9/f9c3a22a56839165bfd6c3b05f78ca25053553532063192836d0332d2b070436.png)\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\nx-y=0\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{1},$ which is the entire plane ${R}^{2}$ with the line $y=x$ removed:\n\n![](images/a1/a1ef93cc90bdd59b922723c2c2aa2bd670b43e416923892c9db53b36ac6b7e57.png)\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $F$ is given by ${D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2},$ shown below.\n\n![](images/f7/f70cab5ec58e63e1751faa65b575a20da748b3e8ee90cffac8f1f3b029bbbd79.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398235 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a308d91e-d998-5724-b79b-60e732336f24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x<{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398237} {:answer/id #uuid "c7ad4611-2a25-5515-9a96-aa614194aed2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x>0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398238} {:answer/id #uuid "185f7409-0599-54ba-a0cd-ab79287fc960" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x>{y}^{2},\\,x\\ne 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398239} {:answer/id #uuid "c6f293d2-0d9e-5145-8206-ea5780ccb464" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x>{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398240} {:answer/id #uuid "689f2980-5408-56a2-a1df-2b0d8b222552" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x>{y}^{2},\\,x>1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398241}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6f293d2-0d9e-5145-8206-ea5780ccb464" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x>{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398240} :answer-field/id #uuid "48ae7e1a-6a16-5754-8819-ca18faede750" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398236}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f9d1b0c7-3a1c-573d-9729-ab5857b0ca01" :question/math-academy-id "q-122663" :question/problem "Determine the domain of the vector field $F$ on ${R}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-{y}^{2})\\,i+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}){e}^{1/x}\\,j\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-{y}^{2}),\\,Q(x,y)=({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}){e}^{1/x}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $F:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the logarithm is positive. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\nx-{y}^{2}>0\\,⟹\\,x>{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x>{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator of the exponent vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\nx=0\\,⟹\\,x=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x>{y}^{2}}\\,.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain of a Vector Field"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172087 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171893 :question/id #uuid "61c3506a-5e4e-557f-bb8c-077170e93204" :question/math-academy-id "e-9468" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on $\\mathbb R^3?$\n\n- $\\mathbf R(x,y,z) = 2\\,\\mathbf i - x\\,\\mathbf j + y\\,\\mathbf k$\n\n- $\\mathbf S(x,y) = 2x\\,\\mathbf i - y^3\\,\\mathbf j$\n\n- $\\mathbf T(x,y,z) = \\dfrac{x}{z}\\,\\mathbf i - \\dfrac{y}{z}\\,\\mathbf j + \\dfrac{z}{2}\\,\\mathbf k$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^3.$ A vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ is a function $\\mathbf F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $\\mathbf R(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns every point $(x,y,z) \\in \\mathbb{R^3}$ to a vector in $\\mathbb{R^3}.$\n\n- The function $\\mathbf S(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns points in $\\mathbb R^2$ (not $\\mathbb R^3$) to vectors in $\\mathbb R^2$ (not $\\mathbb R^3$).\n\n- The function $\\mathbf T(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS = \\big\\{(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3\\:|\\: z \\neq 0\\big\\}\n$$\n\nto a vector in $\\mathbb R^3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb34fdb0-198e-507d-a944-9be782b4cfeb" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172279 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d015c74-c5e0-5aef-8e09-1283f57ea5af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254199} {:answer/id #uuid "affd561e-af4d-58e2-bfbe-6320f0af0e42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255217} {:answer/id #uuid "cc771a9b-2a75-5e58-aac2-d970f8b52f78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255488} {:answer/id #uuid "d7127032-5df6-55a6-9301-b047e0321c13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255574} {:answer/id #uuid "d7d49e1c-5bde-5ec4-858a-e260cc5c430c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255582}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "affd561e-af4d-58e2-bfbe-6320f0af0e42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255217} :answer-field/id #uuid "5870a3e5-dc79-5eea-83fe-4894d64a3cdc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256091}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "060c20fc-1491-5624-9b50-89ab0dfe4d22" :question/math-academy-id "q-122467" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n- $G(t)={e}^{t}\\,i-t\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k$\n- $H(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i-{y}^{2}\\,j$\n- $K(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,i-y\\,j+z\\,k$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $G(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in $R$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $H(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\n- The function $K(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}\\,|\\,x\\ne n\\pi ,\\,n\\in Z}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172285 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1acf9295-80b0-516b-96f6-ea57ff418e00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253899} {:answer/id #uuid "6341e0f1-f66f-533c-8750-dfabab18ba1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254561} {:answer/id #uuid "65f0a3f4-ea32-5d95-ad27-aa9702523a1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254583} {:answer/id #uuid "ad9f4d9c-d4cb-5c25-8f52-0736b7c72fd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255202} {:answer/id #uuid "cf1bc398-9956-525c-883f-70b34e987e00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6341e0f1-f66f-533c-8750-dfabab18ba1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254561} :answer-field/id #uuid "4eda6f1e-9be0-5856-a2d2-8abe38bb2111" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256076}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4052d0d0-adca-5eb3-9bef-49295b1e5651" :question/math-academy-id "q-122697" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n- $U(x,y,z)=⟨x+2,\\sqrt{x-y},\\operatorname{ln}⁡z⟩$\n- $V(x,y)=⟨x{e}^{y},1-y⟩$\n- $W(t)=⟨{t}^{2},2t,2-3t⟩$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $U(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}\\,|\\,x\\ge y,\\,z>0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $V(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\n- The function $W(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in $R$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256694 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a0e2775-dae5-5363-a311-a301ca37db9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253890} {:answer/id #uuid "530f8e39-cb60-5d6f-9021-1268081d55d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254396} {:answer/id #uuid "54c2ae82-1e33-50b6-9b45-93678c89f1fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254409} {:answer/id #uuid "63c6f01c-573e-5a49-ba25-157919c201cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254565} {:answer/id #uuid "9062563e-0f92-548e-b519-03e516bdf7b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254938}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54c2ae82-1e33-50b6-9b45-93678c89f1fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254409} :answer-field/id #uuid "28fa9082-3c30-531c-bc27-0dc4d10a64e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255992}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "72c8972f-c700-5c53-9029-c1e143b47247" :question/math-academy-id "q-122685" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n- $T(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,j+{z}^{2}\\,k$\n- $U(t)=2t\\,i-{t}^{2}\\,j-\\sqrt{t}\\,k$\n- $V(x,y,z)={e}^{-2z}\\sqrt{x+y}$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $T(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point $(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}$ to a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $U(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in $R$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $V(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{3}$ to functions in $R$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256695 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39b861f2-69ec-5c65-9508-f7294f45044a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254175} {:answer/id #uuid "72c370f1-172c-5c9f-95ad-985b95828365" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254687} {:answer/id #uuid "770cf01b-45a0-56c4-9961-b8699f72d11f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254724} {:answer/id #uuid "a69d8038-8063-5f8c-8998-13ee7e4f1213" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255138} {:answer/id #uuid "f9a7d368-1751-561c-a4a6-3b4918ec23a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255857}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72c370f1-172c-5c9f-95ad-985b95828365" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254687} :answer-field/id #uuid "ede176c7-0d20-5a1c-a907-6224f17916d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256341}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "09fffcb2-a27e-5da4-b160-3d4f79d02cb0" :question/math-academy-id "q-122688" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n- $D(x,y,z)=5\\,i+{z}^{2}\\,j+(y+x)\\,k$\n- $E(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i-\\sqrt{y}\\,j$\n- $G(x,y,z)=x(y+1)\\,i+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(y-z)\\,j+{z}^{3}\\,k$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $D(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point $(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}$ to a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $E(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\n- The function $G(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}\\,|\\,y-z\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\\,n\\in Z}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256696 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ad7b008-31c5-5bbf-abcb-fdeabdee0637" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254050} {:answer/id #uuid "3590623b-9e50-5848-b85f-bcc80ccdb0b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254128} {:answer/id #uuid "7aa18df4-7b1c-5773-aa27-4212c519143d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254760} {:answer/id #uuid "bd4e8ead-f681-56ea-81b5-01bb58856d8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255364} {:answer/id #uuid "d1088b67-b04f-5098-835c-4c978287cdc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255535}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7aa18df4-7b1c-5773-aa27-4212c519143d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254760} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf679d2e-39bb-5004-a950-b3c63edce100" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256292}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6d3cf160-5814-5830-a8cc-6fc64892cdce" :question/math-academy-id "q-122691" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n- $U(x,y,z)=(x+2){e}^{yz}$\n- $V(x,y,z)=⟨5{e}^{-{z}^{2}},{x}^{3}y,1-{y}^{3}⟩$\n- $W(x,y)=⟨x-3{y}^{2},2-y⟩$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $U(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{3}$ to functions in $R$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\n- The function $V(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point $(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}$ to a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $W(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256698 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "197b292e-1ccc-5a2a-bbd3-69631cf5cdfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253886} {:answer/id #uuid "4282ef58-c196-533e-a6c5-65a78e4167e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254237} {:answer/id #uuid "72371778-9fa6-55d9-a8c5-e39c0c1c76f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254682} {:answer/id #uuid "81960d80-10de-550c-8912-2cf1586a9e49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254816} {:answer/id #uuid "eed3a33f-1dad-5c80-bfdb-c8efe6f43c69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255760}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eed3a33f-1dad-5c80-bfdb-c8efe6f43c69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255760} :answer-field/id #uuid "74382c67-5fd5-5692-9423-b4141bf3106f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256135}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "659f4527-cc88-5c6b-8485-f595eee1689f" :question/math-academy-id "q-122694" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n- $N(x,y)=⟨x{e}^{y},\\sqrt{x+y}⟩$\n- $P(x,y,z)=⟨{e}^{x-2y},\\sqrt{3-x},3{z}^{3}⟩$\n- $Q(x,y,z)=⟨-1,2,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x-y+z)⟩$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $N(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\n- The function $P(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}\\,|\\,3-x\\ge 0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $Q(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}\\,|\\,2x-y+z>0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256699 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32dc221c-9d3c-5ba5-b8f8-5f66cc98c204" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254109} {:answer/id #uuid "83efb2f1-1659-564d-b42b-e819c7758c66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254843} {:answer/id #uuid "9493d975-f804-58ac-949a-37a70ebd19d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254975} {:answer/id #uuid "9e63abe1-bc16-5e58-8947-238f06b6a38a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255064} {:answer/id #uuid "db43c005-6e48-5fe7-a0a7-8355e9b574ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255615}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83efb2f1-1659-564d-b42b-e819c7758c66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254843} :answer-field/id #uuid "61039999-72de-5aa0-b04f-7ff4d69d025f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256102}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "89ed8a40-4bb8-5a40-9a2c-1bf03637a8bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-122696" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n- $C(x,y,z)=\\frac{z}{x+y}$\n- $D(x,y)=⟨{y}^{3}-3,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-y),7{x}^{3}⟩$\n- $E(x,y,z)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+z),\\operatorname{tan}⁡z,\\frac{1}{x-y}⟩$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $C(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{3}$ to functions in $R$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\n- The function $D(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $E(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}\\,|\\,x\\ne y,\\,z\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\\,n\\in Z}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380692 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e1241cb-e0b2-5b46-8f65-a3c4c3647e6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380694} {:answer/id #uuid "81271eb6-6f13-53c5-8aa9-8681bb11bee7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380695} {:answer/id #uuid "cd3f72cb-d68f-5920-a038-03163c1fef91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380696} {:answer/id #uuid "2e773405-32c0-5805-81d2-1ac74b422fb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380697} {:answer/id #uuid "2ce4f059-c075-5696-ac0c-73923ae49957" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380698}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ce4f059-c075-5696-ac0c-73923ae49957" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380698} :answer-field/id #uuid "26e476e2-9bb5-503b-95c4-0d2fc7849f6d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380693}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b17979a9-57b7-5c2d-8065-c277267e307e" :question/math-academy-id "q-122686" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n- $P(x,y)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,i+(x+y)^{2}\\,j$\n- $Q(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i-\\frac{x}{y}\\,j+\\frac{1}{z+1}\\,k$\n- $R(t)=\\frac{1}{t+1}\\,i-(3t-1)\\,j+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,k$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\n- The function $Q(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}\\,|\\,z\\ne -1,\\,y\\ne 0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $R(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in $R$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380699 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7ef600e-3fa7-5dd9-a9fc-2b417fe03e74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380701} {:answer/id #uuid "5e6ec70d-19a0-5b3e-bfe1-e05b2b422ef3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380702} {:answer/id #uuid "75de271f-3b44-5352-b40a-86d6328346d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380703} {:answer/id #uuid "49cd7923-2c07-57ef-bd86-d4b6461a7cf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380704} {:answer/id #uuid "f5e3f847-6c34-58ac-ab31-4ffb21d98290" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380705}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e6ec70d-19a0-5b3e-bfe1-e05b2b422ef3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380702} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fa44fa7-67a0-5e32-9d16-0c65707b3d44" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380700}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "438dc864-2346-5ec7-8b61-791bfb4d3c82" :question/math-academy-id "q-122695" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n- $M(x,y,z)=⟨z-{x}^{2},3x+{z}^{2},\\frac{1}{x+y}⟩$\n- $N(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+1,1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x⟩$\n- $P(t)=⟨{t}^{3},\\sqrt{1-t}\\,⟩$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $M(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}\\,|\\,x+y\\ne 0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $N(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\n- The function $P(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in $R$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields on Three-Dimensions"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172088 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171894 :question/id #uuid "b226c391-9538-59ff-85ff-dde4d73f209b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9473" :question/problem "![](images/de/de305db47bcfae58b75797e88ae4a7c7825174bd58d77991ba8dfb4c0b8a13cb.png)\n\n \n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?\n\n- $\\mathbf  F(x,y,z) = \\mathbf j + \\mathbf k$\n\n- $\\mathbf  G(x,y,z) = \\mathbf k$\n\n- $\\mathbf  H(x,y,z) = z\\,\\mathbf k$\n\n- $\\mathbf  K(x,y,z) = -\\mathbf k$" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $z$-axis. So, the $\\mathbf i$ and $\\mathbf j$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \\mathbf j + \\mathbf k\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *negative* $z$-direction, so the $\\mathbf k$ component of our vector field is negative. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf G(x,y,z) = \\mathbf k\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $\\mathbf i$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf H(x,y,z) = z\\,\\mathbf k\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $\\mathbf K(x,y,z) = -\\mathbf k.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "888aa30d-7179-507c-a30c-38209587333b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172282 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0af393b5-0b0a-51ee-963e-9232b624b8d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "G(x,y,z)=-i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253755} {:answer/id #uuid "4ebe45a6-6f14-5783-a2c5-0fff74a623ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "K(x,y,z)={e}^{x}\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254352} {:answer/id #uuid "5a235ec0-b137-57da-bcd7-fa7e7f95a670" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "H(x,y,z)=i+j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254460} {:answer/id #uuid "6c27c496-3691-52cb-89b6-a510f5ec6427" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F(x,y,z)=i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254631} {:answer/id #uuid "99ad8cef-0db5-5109-bbb4-eb38f07c8dc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255028}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c27c496-3691-52cb-89b6-a510f5ec6427" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F(x,y,z)=i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254631} :answer-field/id #uuid "1226930a-6b24-57fb-a5e7-1829039d9728" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255946}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "505eb075-afb5-5225-b846-447773bcc89d" :question/math-academy-id "q-122559" :question/problem "![](images/cf/cfc15b6202167211b7d6c0e8f6635dd9e571810d5296a209a4c317350604c4a4.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $x$-axis. So, the $j$ and $k$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nH(x,y,z)=i+j+k,\\,\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *positive* $x$-direction, so the $i$ component of our vector field is positive. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nG(x,y,z)=-i\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $i$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nK(x,y,z)={e}^{x}\\,i\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $F(x,y,z)=i.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172284 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48f0b5a2-3be5-594f-a754-99c98d96988e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254295} {:answer/id #uuid "c4981513-4d32-54f0-884a-7cfa95b9c2fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "H(x,y,z)=-k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255427} {:answer/id #uuid "d6dc3e8c-9e7f-50a4-b937-a99d9f06d7ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "G(x,y,z)=i-k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255572} {:answer/id #uuid "da52d501-6db0-512c-804d-9bbd6ce2ad8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255607} {:answer/id #uuid "fbbc53f3-143a-5ed2-b73c-2af527f6a4cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "K(x,y,z)=2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255881}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fbbc53f3-143a-5ed2-b73c-2af527f6a4cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "K(x,y,z)=2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255881} :answer-field/id #uuid "a548685d-413d-5f58-8841-0b64dd56f823" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256224}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0777fbfe-65b4-5afe-92ce-361fbbf5b37f" :question/math-academy-id "q-122664" :question/problem "![](images/7a/7a0a919af689e9c332ba6ceb3d606208dab9eabadef9b4871ac8e164843ebe88.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $z$-axis. So, the $i$ and $j$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nG(x,y,z)=i-k\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *positive* $z$-direction, so the $k$ component of our vector field is positive. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nH(x,y,z)=-k\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $k$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,k\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $K(x,y,z)=2\\,k.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256686 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f6c3873-4d1d-574a-a9c2-8b7237391726" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F(x,y,z)=i+x\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253944} {:answer/id #uuid "77a79fac-2c83-5559-8e88-df7821819c38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254728} {:answer/id #uuid "7cc3b387-dc15-5110-8f89-a3ced8863a54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "G(x,y,z)=-i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254776} {:answer/id #uuid "a7b92edb-bdc2-5822-8401-8722052a64cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "K(x,y,z)=2\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255150} {:answer/id #uuid "efac23a6-983f-5fcf-b670-3454bacfa6f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "H(x,y,z)=x\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255764}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7b92edb-bdc2-5822-8401-8722052a64cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "K(x,y,z)=2\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255150} :answer-field/id #uuid "41312e99-823c-57ca-bbea-af3327d42ce6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256044}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b83bfdca-4e4b-517b-aad8-aed17555d2d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-122651" :question/problem "![](images/7c/7cb4dd61f9d42a48c022a183e7f4310a8a4ed7ae881edd136881ef6863282e2c.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $x$-axis. So, the $j$ and $k$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=i+x\\,j\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *positive* $x$-direction, so the $i$ component of our vector field is positive. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nG(x,y,z)=-i\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $i$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nH(x,y,z)=x\\,i\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $K(x,y,z)=2\\,i.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256689 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "246c2163-a431-53df-9c32-91cc7baa04f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "K(x,y,z)=2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253998} {:answer/id #uuid "664169a1-99e9-5129-a9ac-60cb4c0afd03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "H(x,y,z)=x\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254588} {:answer/id #uuid "66f7f9c9-6005-52af-8503-4204ea104780" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "G(x,y,z)=4\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254599} {:answer/id #uuid "ce110d77-de29-50d0-b4a3-b3cd16e56e9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255501} {:answer/id #uuid "ce8e27bb-e7f4-5967-96b3-21b1a4efd7bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F(x,y,z)=-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255506}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce8e27bb-e7f4-5967-96b3-21b1a4efd7bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F(x,y,z)=-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255506} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf119d84-49c7-595f-9a0e-210df016ca2c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256261}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "db25e593-34c2-554c-af14-c5a728ea4219" :question/math-academy-id "q-122668" :question/problem "![](images/f8/f89653698d9c2bad0c2e6a77d4dad1953a9fd72f89cbfc08fd68aac802acd284.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $y$-axis. So, the $i$ and $k$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nG(x,y,z)=4\\,k\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *negative* $y$-direction, so the $j$ component of our vector field is negative. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nK(x,y,z)=2\\,j\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $j$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nH(x,y,z)=x\\,j\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $F(x,y,z)=-j.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256690 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52540afe-6bae-5db6-95a2-a9867a8f858b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417332} {:answer/id #uuid "52693f37-1a7d-5506-8ee4-6500aa2d7349" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F(x,y,z)=i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417333} {:answer/id #uuid "c2ec2c00-c765-5813-aaa4-789423b68551" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "H(x,y,z)=k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417336} {:answer/id #uuid "f6c9d1db-461c-5c21-9d19-a6e9f1533a90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "G(x,y,z)=-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417339} {:answer/id #uuid "ff5ef832-2edf-5758-ba7c-c1096ad3a8c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "K(x,y,z)=-{x}^{2}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417340}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52540afe-6bae-5db6-95a2-a9867a8f858b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417332} :answer-field/id #uuid "f30ffaea-faee-50f8-bb72-c0bdccf87c9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417342}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "548bbae5-603d-5bc6-9f7d-4ca6dbc971eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-122670" :question/problem "![](images/64/64f02fa7dcd23b20e61801c0453d8fe7ed013936572e1ee63f748361edd4dec5.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $z$-axis. So, the $i$ and $j$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=i,\\,G(x,y,z)=-j\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *negative* $z$-direction, so the $k$ component of our vector field is negative. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nH(x,y,z)=k\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $k$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nK(x,y,z)=-{x}^{2}\\,k\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram doesn't correspond to any of the listed vector fields."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259551 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44832c8e-8988-5ee0-ac8b-67ae439d923f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257381} {:answer/id #uuid "70a0b147-68a3-5ec0-ae90-b9c82057aad3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "H(x,y,z)=-2\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257773} {:answer/id #uuid "96d0c94c-faa8-5d39-b3c3-d13382b0eced" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F(x,y,z)=i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258114} {:answer/id #uuid "acdf99e3-cf16-5362-88ce-860873156446" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "K(x,y,z)=-i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258289} {:answer/id #uuid "f8d361e0-0169-5758-b933-86a6b5b677fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "G(x,y,z)=-y\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258965}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70a0b147-68a3-5ec0-ae90-b9c82057aad3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "H(x,y,z)=-2\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257773} :answer-field/id #uuid "861c8be9-902d-5545-831a-501704ab8be1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259264}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1c63175a-a455-54b8-b784-f9c30e4507e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-123748" :question/problem "![](images/bf/bff60466ca8abba4fd849c046c02af3a4811279774975fc981cafad91d734ed5.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $x$-axis. So, the $j$ and $k$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nK(x,y,z)=-i+j\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *negative* $x$-direction, so the $i$ component of our vector field is negative. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=i\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $i$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nG(x,y,z)=-y\\,i\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $H(x,y,z)=-2\\,i.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259552 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b0b6380-f4e9-5ec2-bcc7-03b4ff06fa3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "H(x,y,z)=i+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258003} {:answer/id #uuid "908d4a7b-6af1-5afd-acfb-b46fa54f9edb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "G(x,y,z)=-{x}^{2}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258053} {:answer/id #uuid "9abc8af7-a3e2-5316-8e7c-f13306f668fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F(x,y,z)=j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258141} {:answer/id #uuid "9e8f5f06-c805-518c-a5e2-55a319c28965" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258177} {:answer/id #uuid "fd71ba70-4ea4-572f-abeb-acc8c1e00583" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "K(x,y,z)=i+{e}^{x}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258998}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e8f5f06-c805-518c-a5e2-55a319c28965" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258177} :answer-field/id #uuid "215c8d02-cce0-5acf-9590-b24a831a9a6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259091}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "589ce8fd-18ca-5044-bdd6-238376c16f16" :question/math-academy-id "q-123749" :question/problem "![](images/f8/f89653698d9c2bad0c2e6a77d4dad1953a9fd72f89cbfc08fd68aac802acd284.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $y$-axis. So, the $i$ and $k$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\nH(x,y,z)=i+k,\\,K(x,y,z)=i+{e}^{x}\\,k\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *negative* $y$-direction, so the $j$ component of our vector field is negative. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=j\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $j$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nG(x,y,z)=-{x}^{2}\\,j\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram doesn't correspond to any of the listed vector fields."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259553 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0858fdee-1e5e-5d7f-ab1e-3a89c550e232" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "H(x,y,z)=-\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256850} {:answer/id #uuid "7ce989ee-22a0-54e3-b954-3393774f7d61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "K(x,y,z)=-{e}^{y}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257884} {:answer/id #uuid "accb76c2-2fde-50e7-8f9d-57f87a826ee3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258288} {:answer/id #uuid "b7d26cd8-6acb-55c4-826a-fbc8652f0dd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "G(x,y,z)=i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258388} {:answer/id #uuid "ed5f02df-9d28-5ae8-8784-9a8977313908" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F(x,y,z)=-2\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258833}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "accb76c2-2fde-50e7-8f9d-57f87a826ee3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258288} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3c30536-6d13-54e1-9db1-ae7eaa848eaa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259319}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c9c83b42-2a92-533d-be7a-98b1b72e544d" :question/math-academy-id "q-123750" :question/problem "![](images/cf/cfc15b6202167211b7d6c0e8f6635dd9e571810d5296a209a4c317350604c4a4.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $x$-axis. So, the $j$ and $k$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nG(x,y,z)=i-j\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *positive* $x$-direction, so the $i$ component of our vector field is positive. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=-2\\,i,\\,H(x,y,z)=-\\,i\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $i$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nK(x,y,z)=-{e}^{y}\\,k\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram doesn't correspond to any of the listed vector fields."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371665 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6291ddda-70de-5718-9bfc-105bebc04e63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371667} {:answer/id #uuid "9c909eaf-192d-5156-bfd4-507f1094667b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "H(x,y,z)=i+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371668} {:answer/id #uuid "c96f1d9d-d0ba-5ae3-bcdf-556a56778e37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F(x,y,z)=-2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371669} {:answer/id #uuid "cef6452c-f105-5828-9426-ff34265362da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "G(x,y,z)=3\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371670} {:answer/id #uuid "15bc72b2-4db5-5aa4-9cbf-0714e10fa1a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "K(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371671}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cef6452c-f105-5828-9426-ff34265362da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "G(x,y,z)=3\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371670} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9373a90-9549-57f3-9c97-5391475a1938" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371666}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f6439da6-7b28-559c-9b9e-cf7883c3a650" :question/math-academy-id "q-122662" :question/problem "![](images/8d/8d978a63c661ddb2adfca6f0be5315778abb2464a1e1f45f78dedfa4cbaa5d12.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $y$-axis. So, the $i$ and $k$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nH(x,y,z)=i+k\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *positive* $y$-direction, so the $j$ component of our vector field is positive. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=-2\\,j\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $j$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nK(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $G(x,y,z)=3\\,j.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375804 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a066e71c-60d5-5fbb-80e0-33c201104192" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375806} {:answer/id #uuid "251b33e0-026f-5d55-b464-0243c1951e03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "K(x,y,z)=i-j-k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375807} {:answer/id #uuid "f666e84d-6691-5a63-8975-137451851b83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F(x,y,z)=y\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375808} {:answer/id #uuid "e4e84d30-dd2a-5fa6-ae2d-25fd3bdb8810" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "G(x,y,z)=i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375809} {:answer/id #uuid "11bfffa1-bd3e-551d-accb-96f12bb32b33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "H(x,y,z)=-i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375810}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11bfffa1-bd3e-551d-accb-96f12bb32b33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "H(x,y,z)=-i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375810} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ad7530e-1d52-5779-9ca6-d5f03c6b618e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375805}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "76f85577-2f56-52c7-abc0-220aedba2d13" :question/math-academy-id "q-122667" :question/problem "![](images/9d/9db14c13d03cb99846a40622a9633f229e8bd18942f2d03635961529e6970771.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $x$-axis. So, the $j$ and $k$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nK(x,y,z)=i-j-k\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *negative* $x$-direction, so the $i$ component of our vector field is negative. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nG(x,y,z)=i\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $i$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=y\\,i\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $H(x,y,z)=-i.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Three-Dimensional Vector Field"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172130 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171710 :question/id #uuid "38dc8ce4-246d-54bd-a400-48c801d9387f" :question/math-academy-id "e-12344" :question/problem "![](images/1e/1ef5ddc89d7846d1126c60f34fc400669d23d6ca16c2b622addaf2916955ed27.svg)\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z) = z-y$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\big\\langle u^2, \\: -v, \\: v \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v) \\in D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2,$ where $u \\geq 0.$ What is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $\\| \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\| = 2\\sqrt{2}u.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\left\\|  \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\right\\|  \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\| = 2\\sqrt{2}u.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(z-y)\\,{|}_{x={u}^{2}\\,y=-v\\,z=v} \\\\  & =v-(-v) \\\\  & =2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,4\\sqrt{2}uv\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5256297-c928-54e3-bf4d-35f5734f5632" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172375 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9be70133-e143-555a-b316-c857cb78e325" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u^{2}v^{2}\\sqrt{u^{2} + v^{2} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169114} {:answer/id #uuid "a6c62bb4-f2b7-5a28-bab7-5df3a9504149" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "uv\\sqrt{u^{2} + v^{2} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169115} {:answer/id #uuid "cdf2f94a-1a8b-5d99-8a70-f07f6c82f8fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u^{2}v^{2}\\sqrt{uv}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169116} {:answer/id #uuid "0966e2fa-eeb2-5012-9af2-d57f5e7b0e1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u^{2}v^{2}\\sqrt{u + v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169117} {:answer/id #uuid "2e1bec9b-3572-5c9b-a9e1-b8bf0036f7d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "uv\\sqrt{uv}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169118}] :answer-field/id #uuid "8b5413c5-cd70-5f06-8ea5-ec7c6d11e516" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169113} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c6491ad-31df-5c44-8510-45289f4f4d41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{2}{v}^{2}\\sqrt{u+v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401201} {:answer/id #uuid "ae2a58c0-318a-559b-8de4-48cf1599122b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{2}{v}^{2}\\sqrt{uv}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401202} {:answer/id #uuid "66a3fc66-6953-52e2-8b17-93c3ff13ddf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "uv\\sqrt{uv}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401203} {:answer/id #uuid "c9fed922-03a1-59c5-b2d8-bcf5a4514733" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "uv\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401204} {:answer/id #uuid "ecef412c-1fbd-55f7-a2eb-dcc4e3d8418d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{2}{v}^{2}\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecef412c-1fbd-55f7-a2eb-dcc4e3d8418d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{2}{v}^{2}\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401205} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b5472ad-00ad-5af8-bfaf-97021ee46355" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401200}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "25e44fde-444d-5880-ba2e-43dd4bc039db" :question/math-academy-id "q-160711" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=xyz$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,uv⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,v⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & v \\\\ 0 & 1 & u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & v \\\\ 1 & u\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & v \\\\ 0 & u\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-v,\\,-u,\\,1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨-v,\\,-u,\\,1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-v)^{2}+(-u)^{2}+(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(xyz)\\,{|}_{x=u\\,y=v\\,z=uv} \\\\  & =u\\cdot v\\cdot uv \\\\  & ={u}^{2}{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,{u}^{2}{v}^{2}\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}+1}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172376 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a6318e5-3878-5d26-b61b-739d349729a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({u}^{2}+u)v\\sqrt{1+8{u}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275566} {:answer/id #uuid "2913e3a7-4834-5ae0-8725-8cf0ddd19bbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({u}^{2}+uv+{u}^{2})v\\sqrt{1+8{u}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275848} {:answer/id #uuid "4635f4d0-2e93-54c6-a194-c7414540fea3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})v\\sqrt{1+8{u}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276098} {:answer/id #uuid "8e7629cd-993c-5b5b-a5b5-4827bb2f0380" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{u}^{2}{v}^{2}\\sqrt{1+8{u}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276769} {:answer/id #uuid "fa861672-33f6-547e-b490-0aa338df3abc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(u+v)v\\sqrt{1+8{u}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277670}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a6318e5-3878-5d26-b61b-739d349729a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({u}^{2}+u)v\\sqrt{1+8{u}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275566} :answer-field/id #uuid "758915d5-fc00-56c4-ac7b-2a8b40d5ac5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277935}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a99cdea7-3ae5-5a3e-abca-eaac3de706cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-160712" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=x+y+z$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨{u}^{2},\\,u-{v}^{2},\\,{v}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=2v\\sqrt{1+8{u}^{2}}.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(x+y+z)\\,{|}_{x={u}^{2}\\,y=u-{v}^{2}\\,z={v}^{2}} \\\\  & ={u}^{2}+(u-{v}^{2})+{v}^{2} \\\\  & ={u}^{2}+u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,2({u}^{2}+u)v\\sqrt{1+8{u}^{2}}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278393 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "407b054a-ad2a-5003-b975-08ebe629a652" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({v}^{3}+2v)\\sqrt{16{u}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276038} {:answer/id #uuid "4c0afc67-2eaf-50ff-a740-16da7f2c7971" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({u}^{3}+{v}^{3})\\sqrt{16{v}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276147} {:answer/id #uuid "533273b3-79e7-5ab4-8e9c-12dbf680ea3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({v}^{2}+2u)\\sqrt{16{u}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276214} {:answer/id #uuid "6262307b-481e-5435-ad06-56dd718d0e29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(u+v)\\sqrt{16{u}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276366} {:answer/id #uuid "ae06b05f-02ef-5a1f-88b5-160bedb18ca3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({u}^{3}+2v)\\sqrt{16{u}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277041}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae06b05f-02ef-5a1f-88b5-160bedb18ca3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({u}^{3}+2v)\\sqrt{16{u}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277041} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e029e9e-eb95-5dc6-b173-809d27542530" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277920}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0739fb8b-67dd-5c96-8b51-b9fe22150a63" :question/math-academy-id "q-161132" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=xy+z$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,{u}^{2},\\,2v⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=\\sqrt{16{u}^{2}+4}$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=\\sqrt{16{u}^{2}+4}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(xy+z)\\,{|}_{x=u\\,y={u}^{2}\\,z=2v} \\\\  & =u.{u}^{2}+2v \\\\  & ={u}^{3}+2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,({u}^{3}+2v)\\sqrt{16{u}^{2}+4}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278394 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40de9f5d-9bb8-505f-856c-ce2c6913769b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{8}\\,{u}^{3}{v}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276043} {:answer/id #uuid "9a30988f-5c27-5ce0-8684-641402fd6ea6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{8}\\,{u}^{4}{v}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276861} {:answer/id #uuid "c2412b0e-122f-55ad-9a36-81cdc271d082" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{8}\\,{u}^{2}{v}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277204} {:answer/id #uuid "d49654d0-756c-5b91-9e96-98d9917a2ace" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{8}\\,{u}^{3}{v}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277349} {:answer/id #uuid "ea08dbc3-4809-5054-a017-c2a23b383426" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{8}\\,{u}^{5}{v}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277522}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea08dbc3-4809-5054-a017-c2a23b383426" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{8}\\,{u}^{5}{v}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277522} :answer-field/id #uuid "e911c170-af03-582c-ad47-4ba84716458b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278135}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3771f844-0efb-5152-bb73-0315652e05d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-161134" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨{u}^{2},\\,v,\\,v⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=\\sqrt{8}u.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=\\sqrt{8}u.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =({x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2})\\,{|}_{x={u}^{2}\\,y=v\\,z=v} \\\\  & =({u}^{2}\\cdot {u}^{2})\\cdot {v}^{2}\\cdot {v}^{2}\\, \\\\  & ={u}^{4}{v}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\sqrt{8}\\,{u}^{5}{v}^{4}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278395 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60908e82-b692-599f-82b9-892161866329" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-{u}^{2}+3(u+v){v}^{2})\\sqrt{8{v}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276341} {:answer/id #uuid "708462c8-f5ae-5b10-8bc2-cbaa9b1a5f89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-{u}^{2}-3(u-v){v}^{2})\\sqrt{8{v}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276492} {:answer/id #uuid "7755f123-28ec-560e-a4c0-0541364afe71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({u}^{2}-3(u+v){v}^{2})\\sqrt{8{v}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276558} {:answer/id #uuid "7f3e8ea1-6b77-5aea-ae14-47ac28753c57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-{u}^{2}-3({u}^{2}+v){v}^{2})\\sqrt{8{v}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276632} {:answer/id #uuid "a35498bf-e5d4-5d74-b5ee-fd684180144f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({u}^{2}+3(u+v){v}^{2})\\sqrt{8{v}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276944}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60908e82-b692-599f-82b9-892161866329" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-{u}^{2}+3(u+v){v}^{2})\\sqrt{8{v}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276341} :answer-field/id #uuid "eebba4c6-890d-5236-a8b1-f85351eb3247" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278146}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "af99ff54-0eba-5a3e-990a-a1878cebaaed" :question/math-academy-id "q-161136" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=-{x}^{2}+3yz$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,u+v,\\,{v}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=\\sqrt{8{v}^{2}+1}.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=\\sqrt{8{v}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(-{x}^{2}+3yz)\\,{|}_{x=u\\,y=u+v\\,z={v}^{2}} \\\\  & =-(u\\cdot u)+3\\cdot (u+v)\\cdot ({v}^{2}) \\\\  & =-{u}^{2}+3(u+v){v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(-{u}^{2}+3(u+v){v}^{2})\\sqrt{8{v}^{2}+1}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278462 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02421d75-88be-52d7-b6a4-7091fc5bc066" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({u}^{2}{v}^{2}-u)\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275497} {:answer/id #uuid "37a3b016-ad87-55b8-92c0-56d4b344f479" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(uv-u)\\sqrt{2u}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275970} {:answer/id #uuid "4f430a34-697b-5af8-a53f-71af555ef03e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{2}v\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276184} {:answer/id #uuid "67494c52-64b3-56cb-b6c1-1db2880ed8b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({u}^{2}{v}^{2}+2)\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276402} {:answer/id #uuid "f4d8f707-b205-57cd-849c-7a0bdc24dc3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(u{v}^{2}+u)\\sqrt{2u}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277619}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02421d75-88be-52d7-b6a4-7091fc5bc066" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({u}^{2}{v}^{2}-u)\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275497} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc456dfe-3c1e-58f1-9083-307a9c6e88a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278111}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d149ed70-6a6c-529d-a7c4-ab22e040de29" :question/math-academy-id "q-161285" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{z}^{2}+y$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨-u,\\,-u,\\,-v⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =({x}^{2}{z}^{2}+y)\\,{|}_{x=-u\\,y=-u\\,z=-v} \\\\  & =(-u)^{2}\\cdot (-v)^{2}-u \\\\  & ={u}^{2}{v}^{2}-u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,({u}^{2}{v}^{2}-u)\\sqrt{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278463 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e8eb946-bade-52e7-a628-5f87cb28e35c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({u}^{2}+{v}^{2}+u)\\sqrt{8v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275600} {:answer/id #uuid "12277fa3-1146-5aca-a0ac-a0f443b4ecd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({v}^{2}-{u}^{2}-2)\\sqrt{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275643} {:answer/id #uuid "905035c0-ecb9-5958-a0aa-abb13fc99ee9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})\\sqrt{8}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276783} {:answer/id #uuid "aa693649-bf12-5748-af20-c60686051053" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({u}^{2}-{v}^{2}-2u)\\sqrt{8}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277010} {:answer/id #uuid "f72b9498-2b49-5473-af2a-31b5ee8fb2c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({u}^{2}+2v+2u)\\sqrt{8}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277637}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa693649-bf12-5748-af20-c60686051053" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({u}^{2}-{v}^{2}-2u)\\sqrt{8}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277010} :answer-field/id #uuid "67ccc538-716e-5741-af32-020559500c0f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277908}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d9e295ad-a9d0-5267-be9d-f4d887cdb7cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-161286" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=-x-y-z$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,{v}^{2}-{u}^{2},\\,u⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({v}^{2}-{u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-2u,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({v}^{2}-{u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2v,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -2u & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2v & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}-2u & 1 \\\\ 2v & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2u \\\\ 0 & 2v\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2u,\\,0,\\,2v⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨-2v,\\,0,\\,2v⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2v)^{2}+(0)^{2}+(2v)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{v}^{2}+4{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(-x-y-z)\\,{|}_{x=u\\,y={v}^{2}-{u}^{2}\\,z=u} \\\\  & =-u-({v}^{2}-{u}^{2})-u \\\\  & ={u}^{2}-{v}^{2}-2u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,({u}^{2}-{v}^{2}-2u)\\sqrt{8}v\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278464 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6af8c537-e57b-5716-8d12-63fcd367dcd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(uv+uv)\\sqrt{2{v}^{2}+4{v}^{4}-{u}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276437} {:answer/id #uuid "764eb8f7-0869-594c-952a-68e04921067e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(v-{u}^{2}{v}^{2})\\sqrt{4{v}^{2}+2{v}^{4}+u}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276545} {:answer/id #uuid "7b524c5d-049b-59c5-aed7-b77cbc36dee4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(u+{u}^{2}{v}^{2})\\sqrt{{v}^{2}-4{v}^{4}-{u}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276595} {:answer/id #uuid "dafd65c0-be97-5117-b59c-70e2892517b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(uv+{u}^{2}{v}^{2})\\sqrt{4{v}^{2}+4{v}^{4}+{u}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277396} {:answer/id #uuid "fe7356ca-baea-56e4-9138-a82a9af96807" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(uv+u{v}^{2})\\sqrt{4{v}^{2}+{v}^{2}+{v}^{2}{u}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277711}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dafd65c0-be97-5117-b59c-70e2892517b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(uv+{u}^{2}{v}^{2})\\sqrt{4{v}^{2}+4{v}^{4}+{u}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277396} :answer-field/id #uuid "04191987-1090-56e3-a6b5-b166d86bb243" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277733}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "616b2956-33ca-5451-b2d1-058d45da4207" :question/math-academy-id "q-161290" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=x-{y}^{2}z$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨uv,\\,-u,\\,-{v}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(uv),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-{v}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨v,\\,-1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(uv),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-{v}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨u,\\,0,\\,-2v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ v & -1 & 0 \\\\ u & 0 & -2v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2v\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}v & 0 \\\\ u & -2v\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}v & -1 \\\\ u & 0\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2v,\\,2{v}^{2},\\,u⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨2v,\\,2{v}^{2},\\,u⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2v)^{2}+(2{v}^{2})^{2}+(u)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{v}^{2}+4{v}^{4}+{u}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(x-{y}^{2}z)\\,{|}_{x=uv\\,y=-u\\,z=-{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =uv-(-u)^{2}\\cdot -{v}^{2} \\\\  & =uv+{u}^{2}{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(uv+{u}^{2}{v}^{2})\\sqrt{4{v}^{2}+4{v}^{4}+{u}^{2}}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414460 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31c23b1e-7cd7-5e7a-8c99-254c33c9229b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414434} {:answer/id #uuid "b146a4db-c677-57ea-92f8-07f41239596d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}{u}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414445} {:answer/id #uuid "b723dafe-f5a6-5a70-be14-953187b88dde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414446} {:answer/id #uuid "c87217ff-e3d3-5223-ac71-463a0c197918" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}{u}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414448} {:answer/id #uuid "e4f2c71c-8604-5aa1-9ce7-1663a5f2e6db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414453}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b146a4db-c677-57ea-92f8-07f41239596d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}{u}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414445} :answer-field/id #uuid "16fadbea-4516-5df4-9f64-8b7cc353d5a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414455}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "26a444d6-a870-593f-bb03-1c02941c48b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-161124" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,u,\\,v⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1+0} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,{|}_{x=u\\,y=u\\,z=v} \\\\  & ={u}^{2}+{u}^{2} \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,2\\sqrt{2}{u}^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414461 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cd71aa2-2da0-53d3-a18e-57cc7dd66fe2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{2}(-{u}^{3}+{v}^{4})\\sqrt{4uv+u}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414430} {:answer/id #uuid "6a78c70d-c252-5fa1-b3b9-b5253f7112ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{u}^{2}(-{u}^{3}-{v}^{4})\\sqrt{4{v}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414437} {:answer/id #uuid "a93dd97e-74c6-56c8-a6b1-3cdadc7213e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{u}^{2}({u}^{4}-{v}^{3})\\sqrt{4{u}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414443} {:answer/id #uuid "cb42e303-0573-52db-81ce-d57e99e6f761" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{v}^{2}({u}^{2}+{v}^{4})\\sqrt{{v}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414449} {:answer/id #uuid "d505a7da-72d5-5c19-aa46-75ea67cf0bb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(-{u}^{3}-{v}^{4})\\sqrt{4{v}^{3}+u}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414451}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a78c70d-c252-5fa1-b3b9-b5253f7112ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{u}^{2}(-{u}^{3}-{v}^{4})\\sqrt{4{v}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414437} :answer-field/id #uuid "72a815fc-9d19-53b2-b0ae-b65abcc40317" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414456}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2c7beff7-ff72-566b-ae77-d272cca30605" :question/math-academy-id "q-161288" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=-x-{z}^{2}$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨{u}^{3},\\,v,\\,{v}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{3}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({v}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{u}^{2},\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{3}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({v}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,2v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3{u}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2v\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}3{u}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2v\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}3{u}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-6v{u}^{2},\\,3{u}^{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨0,\\,-6v{u}^{2},\\,3{u}^{2}⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(0)^{2}+(-6v{u}^{2})^{2}+(3{u}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36{v}^{2}{u}^{4}+9{u}^{4}} \\\\  & =3{u}^{2}\\sqrt{4{v}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(-x-{z}^{2})\\,{|}_{x={u}^{3}\\,y=v\\,z={v}^{2}} \\\\  & =-{u}^{3}-({v}^{2}\\cdot {v}^{2}) \\\\  & =-{u}^{3}-{v}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,3{u}^{2}(-{u}^{3}-{v}^{4})\\sqrt{4{v}^{2}+1}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418479 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "059c12fe-eab5-50d3-812e-daede28e5fce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-uv-v)\\sqrt{1+4{v}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418466} {:answer/id #uuid "a69adeb6-2c36-5a75-b29d-f701876ba59a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-u{v}^{2}+v)\\sqrt{1+4{v}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418471} {:answer/id #uuid "cbbd9a36-5d62-5a45-a0b6-211a1e349cc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(u{v}^{2}-{v}^{2})\\sqrt{1+4{v}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418473} {:answer/id #uuid "ded9ba33-54e3-5794-92a9-b6db153210e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-u{v}^{2}-v)\\sqrt{1+4{v}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418474} {:answer/id #uuid "e264331f-dc93-54c6-bb93-c8cd29731c14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-u{v}^{2}+{v}^{2})\\sqrt{1+4{v}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418475}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ded9ba33-54e3-5794-92a9-b6db153210e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-u{v}^{2}-v)\\sqrt{1+4{v}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418474} :answer-field/id #uuid "de80caed-f292-5fbc-ae6d-5bf6abf46eda" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418477}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e6c3f387-d028-56cc-8550-337dfde31023" :question/math-academy-id "q-161135" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=xz+y$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,-v,\\,-{v}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=\\sqrt{1+4{v}^{2}}.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=\\sqrt{1+4{v}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(xz+y)\\,{|}_{x=u\\,y=-v\\,z={v}^{2}} \\\\  & =u\\cdot (-{v}^{2})+(-v) \\\\  & =-u{v}^{2}-v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(-u{v}^{2}-v)\\sqrt{1+4{v}^{2}}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419401 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1796399c-8c6d-5f22-bede-36efd1a03f5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "uv\\sqrt{{u}^{2}{v}^{2}+4{v}^{4}+4{u}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419395} {:answer/id #uuid "8e82d031-e34f-52ca-aadf-8e8d59c47bb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(uv+{v}^{2})\\sqrt{16{u}^{2}{v}^{2}+4{v}^{4}+4{u}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419396} {:answer/id #uuid "bdf13d7f-d54d-59f5-9209-239de83443e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(v+{v}^{2})\\sqrt{16{u}^{2}{v}^{2}+4{v}^{2}+4{u}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419397} {:answer/id #uuid "bf1e4b2c-7a9b-5b87-a6e2-85dd90a414fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(uv+{u}^{2})\\sqrt{16{u}^{2}{v}^{2}+4{v}^{2}+4{u}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419398} {:answer/id #uuid "d04576fc-a1ab-5d09-bca6-54a80b70447c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}\\sqrt{{u}^{2}{v}^{2}+4{v}^{4}+4{u}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419399}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e82d031-e34f-52ca-aadf-8e8d59c47bb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(uv+{v}^{2})\\sqrt{16{u}^{2}{v}^{2}+4{v}^{4}+4{u}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419396} :answer-field/id #uuid "39e51a49-d535-578a-8167-c77ca61f8305" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419400}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4970a05a-3173-5c58-8ea3-194b8fc59474" :question/math-academy-id "q-161133" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=x+z$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨uv,\\,{u}^{2},\\,{v}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=\\sqrt{16{u}^{2}{v}^{2}+4{v}^{4}+4{u}^{4}}.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=\\sqrt{16{u}^{2}{v}^{2}+4{v}^{4}+4{u}^{4}}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(x+z)\\,{|}_{x=uv\\,y={u}^{2}\\,z={v}^{2}} \\\\  & =uv+{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(uv+{v}^{2})\\sqrt{16{u}^{2}{v}^{2}+4{v}^{4}+4{u}^{4}}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Surface Integrals Over Parametric Surfaces"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172131 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171711 :question/id #uuid "fce43073-3f48-5e12-a5c0-ecef3a31a57a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12346" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} (x-y)\\: \\textrm{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by the equations\n\n$$\nx = u+v, \\qquad \ny = u-v, \\qquad \nz = v\n$$\n\nfor $0 \\leq u \\leq 3$ and $0 \\leq v \\leq 3.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\left\\|  \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\right\\|  \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\left\\langle u+v, \\: u-v, \\: v\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-1,\\,-2⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨1,\\,-1,\\,-2⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =x-y\\,{|}_{x=u+v\\,y=u-v\\,z=v} \\\\  & =(u+v)-(u-v) \\\\  & =2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we denote \n\n$$\nD = \\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 3, \\: 0 \\leq v \\leq 3\\},\n$$\n\nour surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}(x-y)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,2v\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{D}\\,2v\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}2v\\,\\text{d}v\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{3}1\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\cdot [{v}^{2}]_{0}^{3}\\cdot [u]_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\cdot 9\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =27\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b529430-3406-506c-b67d-c067dfee44d5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172377 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11345332-1583-5403-b382-1f7db9544110" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{70}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414431} {:answer/id #uuid "6ed83581-5e32-5a0e-8a25-178bb99cade4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\sqrt{70}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414439} {:answer/id #uuid "7d00c43b-e899-573d-afa8-6b12ead91cda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\sqrt{70}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414440} {:answer/id #uuid "bc8100ab-c718-514d-8f3c-f28643c256a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{70}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414447} {:answer/id #uuid "d24b3aff-8b91-5bf0-8ab1-f276e0780c98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{70}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414450}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d00c43b-e899-573d-afa8-6b12ead91cda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\sqrt{70}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414440} :answer-field/id #uuid "be382d30-f02e-5329-bcf7-16808280a34b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414459}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "82d5af65-9d79-5b70-b0f6-be91c0743951" :question/math-academy-id "q-160834" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}(2x+y+z)\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨2u-v,\\,3u+2,\\,u+2v⟩\n$$\n\n \nover the domain $D=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,-1\\le v\\le 1\\}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=\\sqrt{70}.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=\\sqrt{70}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(2x+y+z)\\,{|}_{x=2u-v\\,y=3u+2\\,z=u+2v} \\\\  & =2(2u-v)+(3u+2)+(u+2v) \\\\  & =8u+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(8u+2)\\sqrt{70}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{70}{∬}_{D}\\,(8u+2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{70}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(8u+2)\\,\\text{d}u\\cdot {\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\sqrt{70}\\cdot [4{u}^{2}+2u]_{0}^{1}\\cdot [u]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{70}\\cdot 6\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{70}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172378 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5a1c5c4-aaff-5dad-85ae-7c455c4e77b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169132} {:answer/id #uuid "ad5264d6-51e2-571c-a97d-f200747b780d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\sqrt{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169133} {:answer/id #uuid "d262b1ed-6abf-5731-aad7-a59edbd4996a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169134} {:answer/id #uuid "033ce6a7-bfa6-5c47-acc2-cddc0cab717e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169135} {:answer/id #uuid "2814803c-3a0a-59a5-b8f2-9a2b1d57c431" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169136}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad5264d6-51e2-571c-a97d-f200747b780d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\sqrt{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169133} :answer-field/id #uuid "126dc10e-27a9-5d7d-894d-84b3d2d7de05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169131}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "588db1ad-67d5-5fad-b786-e13158fbed5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-160835" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}4xy\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by the equations\n\n$$\nx=u,\\,y=v,\\,z=6-3u-2v\n$$\n\nfor $0\\le u\\le 2$ and $0\\le v\\le 3.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,6-3u-2v⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(6-3u-2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,-3⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(6-3u-2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,\\,2,\\,1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨3,\\,2,\\,1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+4+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =4xy\\,{|}_{x=u\\,y=v\\,z=6-3u-2v} \\\\  & =4uv\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we denote \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 3\\},\n$$\n\nour surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}4xy\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,4uv\\sqrt{14}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{14}{∬}_{D}\\,4uv\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{14}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2u\\,\\text{d}u\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{3}2v\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\sqrt{14}\\cdot [{u}^{2}]_{0}^{2}\\cdot [{v}^{2}]_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{14}\\cdot 4\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =36\\sqrt{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235462 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d0665e53-645a-508e-8a48-66627b805582" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234874}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0665e53-645a-508e-8a48-66627b805582" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234874} :answer-field/id #uuid "2eb05908-2cad-5f19-9002-4431259f7c7c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235021}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1a131946-3c50-5cb9-8c20-5138d22bf7c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-167649" :question/problem "If we denote our surface of revolution by $S,$ then the total electric charge of the shell is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $Q={∬}_{S}^{}d(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nPreviously, we determined that our surface can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr(x,\\theta )=⟨x,\\,2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,x\\in [0,1],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),\n$$\n\nand the norm of the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖=2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(x,\\theta )$ into $d(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(r(x,\\theta )) & =\\frac{x({y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}{\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x((2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})}{\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{x}^{5}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{x}^{5}}{\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}+1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}d(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}d(r(x,\\theta ))‖{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{4{x}^{5}}{\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}+1}}\\cdot 2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,8{x}^{7}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}8{x}^{7}\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =[{x}^{8}]_{0}^{1}\\cdot [\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278391 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "430ab58b-bfb3-5ff3-a26e-50e397241376" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276070} {:answer/id #uuid "5ccc6906-339a-55eb-81c6-e1340f0e3809" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276304} {:answer/id #uuid "982d058a-fa6a-596f-b4ad-7f3c34706a59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276844} {:answer/id #uuid "a9293427-c368-56c5-9cb1-7d5a9b4ac305" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277002} {:answer/id #uuid "c4de9476-c71d-52f0-8f43-c4631b831c7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277228}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ccc6906-339a-55eb-81c6-e1340f0e3809" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276304} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a05d06b-9876-5842-ad9e-eec5778c2d3d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277999}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fb6920aa-28a6-5cb5-a583-f417d82bbe6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-161127" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}{x}^{2}-2yz\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by the equations\n\n$$\nx=u+v,\\,y=u,\\,z=v\n$$\n\nfor $-1\\le u\\le 1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u+v,\\,u,\\,v⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨1,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+-{1}^{2}+-{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & ={x}^{2}-2yz\\,{|}_{x=u+v\\,y=u\\,z=v} \\\\  & =(u+v)^{2}-2uv \\\\  & ={u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we denote \n\n$$\nD={(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nour surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}{x}^{2}-2yz\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,({u}^{2}+{v}^{2})\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\,{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}+u{v}^{2}]}_{u=-1}^{u=1}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{3}+2{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{[\\frac{2}{3}v+\\frac{2}{3}{v}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}(\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{2}{3})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278411 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "057c351a-27e3-59d3-8013-345a5bcfa346" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275525} {:answer/id #uuid "112a6f37-066f-50c9-88b8-4b14bca55e56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275630} {:answer/id #uuid "2a8621e4-8335-55f8-9a71-143f24ae4226" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275866} {:answer/id #uuid "61176df2-a86f-5867-9df7-fe03f3d206d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276352} {:answer/id #uuid "61458edd-83a0-5587-a19b-e0a38ccdc25b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276356}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61458edd-83a0-5587-a19b-e0a38ccdc25b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276356} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b0ae5ad-a7f1-591f-bc08-e13a68747a2b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277844}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f3da3d1c-88b2-5b2c-b1c5-adc3acc48bde" :question/math-academy-id "q-161180" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}(x+2y-z)\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u-v,\\,2u+2v,\\,2v⟩\n$$\n\n \nover the domain $D={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 2}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=6.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=6.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(x+2y-z)\\,{|}_{x=u-v\\,y=2u+2v\\,z=2v} \\\\  & =(u-v)+2(2u+2v)-(2v) \\\\  & =5u+v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(5u+v)\\cdot 6\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =6{∬}_{D}\\,(5u+v)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{2}({\\int }_{0}^{1}(5u+v)\\,\\text{d}u)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{5{u}^{2}}{2}+vu]}_{0}^{1}\\text{d}v \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{5}{2}+v)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =6{[\\frac{5v}{2}+\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =6[5+2] \\\\  & =42.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278412 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ebe36cd-b1cf-5ce2-8a67-8db3468807b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275603} {:answer/id #uuid "18ad7ac0-5137-5083-9463-529f4f7e3804" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275706} {:answer/id #uuid "4891c5fb-95c8-5c1c-a256-07260e4f9797" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276121} {:answer/id #uuid "9e1ef9b7-1bf5-5710-8957-97f3d292a525" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276898} {:answer/id #uuid "c983dee7-7745-56ff-a7b7-c03e844d53b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277265}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18ad7ac0-5137-5083-9463-529f4f7e3804" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275706} :answer-field/id #uuid "92141582-6640-51e0-ab76-f6c1c7dbae93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277984}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c6424aa2-1e21-5507-975b-b73e043b3939" :question/math-academy-id "q-161182" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}6xyz\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,u+v,\\,v⟩\n$$\n\n \nover the domain $D={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=\\sqrt{3}.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =6xyz\\,{|}_{x=u\\,y=u+v\\,z=v} \\\\  & =6(u)(u+v)(v) \\\\  & =6({u}^{2}v+u{v}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,6({u}^{2}v+u{v}^{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\,({u}^{2}v+u{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{2}v+u{v}^{2})\\text{d}u)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{{u}^{3}v}{3}+\\frac{{u}^{2}{v}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{v}{3}+\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2})\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{[\\frac{{v}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{v}^{3}}{6}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}(\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278425 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1edff915-88e9-58d6-841c-692c1f2c3ec6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275757} {:answer/id #uuid "1f390458-0093-558d-b9b3-ca785ff90164" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275761} {:answer/id #uuid "63d3f2e3-cd39-56de-8c0d-2c0e4e41eb44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276381} {:answer/id #uuid "cbc91682-3c42-5615-aa8f-2f96cb4a6fd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277290} {:answer/id #uuid "fbc623d0-26c3-57ab-9f42-45751dbc3aea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277685}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63d3f2e3-cd39-56de-8c0d-2c0e4e41eb44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276381} :answer-field/id #uuid "a91deb21-01ab-5168-9791-852593bfeb6d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278017}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "80e5707a-3b53-57aa-aa8c-7db876d69b93" :question/math-academy-id "q-161209" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}(xy+z)\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by the equations\n\n$$\nx=u+v,\\,y=u-v,\\,z=0\n$$\n\nfor $0\\le u\\le 1$ and $0\\le v\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u+v,\\,u-v,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,-2⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨0,\\,0,\\,-2⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+0+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =xy+z\\,{|}_{x=u+v\\,y=u-v\\,z=0} \\\\  & =(u+v)(u-v) \\\\  & ={u}^{2}-{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we denote \n\n$$\nD={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 2},\n$$\n\nour surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}(xy+z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{∬}_{D}\\,({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}({\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}u)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}-{v}^{2}u]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{1}{3}-{v}^{2})\\text{d}v \\\\  & =2{[\\frac{v}{3}-\\frac{{v}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2[\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{8}{3}] \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278427 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f7d03c6-d0d9-529a-bce7-fcfcb60d6f23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276187} {:answer/id #uuid "6ca921c6-81e9-5022-989e-b96d7ae33f08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276453} {:answer/id #uuid "8b30494f-8e6d-5099-9018-d53a7f2a115d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276740} {:answer/id #uuid "b4db6155-6904-59b9-b3a7-494630d4b44a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277090} {:answer/id #uuid "c41eebb1-ad78-5933-9beb-c7c83212c490" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277221}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4db6155-6904-59b9-b3a7-494630d4b44a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277090} :answer-field/id #uuid "17135030-c131-5443-8329-4345c0e7edd5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277766}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "aaa6b101-413b-5c8f-a474-bfd4f4726f2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-161212" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}2xz\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by the equations\n\n$$\nx=u,\\,y=u+v,\\,z=2v\n$$\n\nfor $0\\le u\\le 2$ and $0\\le v\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,u+v,\\,2v⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,-2,\\,1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨2,\\,-2,\\,1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+4+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =2xz\\,{|}_{x=u\\,y=u+v\\,z=2v} \\\\  & =2u(2v) \\\\  & =4uv\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we denote \n\n$$\nD={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2},\n$$\n\nour surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}2xz\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,4uv\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3{∬}_{D}\\,4uv\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2}2u\\,\\text{d}u\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}2v\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =3\\cdot [{u}^{2}]_{0}^{2}\\cdot [{v}^{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278442 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bfe6a46-a159-59d8-bee6-18072e5661ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "95" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275577} {:answer/id #uuid "5d212cf2-6ea0-53a0-9bcc-5ecd7212b036" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276306} {:answer/id #uuid "76f545bd-11b2-5ae1-929d-c2f25977a30f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276549} {:answer/id #uuid "b08aefb8-1f20-592d-9092-298b802a0a77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "85" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277056} {:answer/id #uuid "b1167d7b-282c-533d-bad4-77099f24e2f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277062}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76f545bd-11b2-5ae1-929d-c2f25977a30f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276549} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6564796-0979-5996-bb99-982fadfe08ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278126}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "81e32a26-4f8e-507c-9b65-da0df62a940b" :question/math-academy-id "q-161242" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}x(y+z)\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by the equations\n\n$$\nx=2u,\\,y=-3v,\\,z=4v\n$$\n\nfor $0\\le u\\le 2$ and $0\\le v\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨2u,\\,-3v,\\,4v⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-3v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(4v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-3v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(4v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-3,\\,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-8,\\,-6⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨0,\\,-8,\\,-6⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-8)^{2}+(-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+64+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{100} \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =x(y+z)\\,{|}_{x=2u\\,y=-3v\\,z=4v} \\\\  & =2u(-3v+4v) \\\\  & =2uv\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we denote \n\n$$\nD={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2},\n$$\n\nour surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}x(y+z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,2uv\\cdot 10\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5{∬}_{D}\\,4uv\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{2}2u\\,\\text{d}u\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}2v\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =5\\cdot [{u}^{2}]_{0}^{2}\\cdot [{v}^{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278443 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "315458db-5834-5453-b924-0dc1201d2a1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "102" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275915} {:answer/id #uuid "3f637c5a-3c45-5126-82aa-7a59ac498fcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276026} {:answer/id #uuid "62f9e58f-ad46-5445-831b-6ee4a956c58f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276373} {:answer/id #uuid "9961fb98-fed5-544f-98af-e57bcc6d74f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "110" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276854} {:answer/id #uuid "f811b651-f27c-5a53-a849-08f534196ba1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-108" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277644}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f811b651-f27c-5a53-a849-08f534196ba1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-108" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277644} :answer-field/id #uuid "4225a37a-d844-5f27-b52c-36d4f1ac1cc0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277828}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c63ddeef-33ef-5328-bfb9-278493d60c61" :question/math-academy-id "q-161245" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}({x}^{2}-{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by the equations\n\n$$\nx=2u+v,\\,y=2u-2v,\\,z=u+2v\n$$\n\nfor $-1\\le u\\le 1$ and $1\\le v\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨2u+v,\\,2u-2v,\\,u+2v⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2u-2v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,2,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2u-2v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-2,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6,\\,-3,\\,-6⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨6,\\,-3,\\,-6⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+(-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36+9+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{81} \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & ={x}^{2}-{z}^{2}\\,{|}_{x=2u+v\\,y=2u-2v\\,z=u+2v} \\\\  & =(2u+v)^{2}-(u+2v)^{2} \\\\  & =3{u}^{2}-3{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we denote \n\n$$\nD={(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,1\\le v\\le 2},\n$$\n\nour surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(3{u}^{2}-3{v}^{2})\\cdot 9\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =9{∬}_{D}\\,(3{u}^{2}-3{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{1}^{2}({\\int }_{-1}^{1}(3{u}^{2}-3{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}u)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{1}^{2}{[{u}^{3}-3{v}^{2}u]}_{-1}^{1}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{1}^{2}(2-6{v}^{2})\\text{d}v \\\\  & =9\\cdot [2v-2{v}^{3}]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =9\\cdot (-12) \\\\  & =-108.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414462 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22129875-cc14-5ad3-8bba-b26995c05188" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414433} {:answer/id #uuid "38973f90-0f85-5e71-8380-79a67500ceed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414435} {:answer/id #uuid "44291a57-8b30-555c-b7e9-95314ffa4f9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414436} {:answer/id #uuid "ab922d47-0766-574c-83bb-4e4ccfbb77ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414444} {:answer/id #uuid "e1fe1620-65f9-5859-8a82-9a5f97b2fafb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414452}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1fe1620-65f9-5859-8a82-9a5f97b2fafb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414452} :answer-field/id #uuid "93abc2ba-af03-5472-9cce-10fe847607a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414458}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d27e0ee0-ad93-5416-8abb-f21167765a8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-161181" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}(3x-2y+3z)\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨2u+v,\\,2u+v,\\,-v⟩\n$$\n\n \nover the domain $D=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,-1\\le v\\le 0\\}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=2\\sqrt{2}.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=2\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(3x-2y+3z)\\,{|}_{x=2u+v\\,y=2u+v\\,z=-v} \\\\  & =3(2u+v)-2(2u+v)+3(-v) \\\\  & =2(u-v)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,2(u-v)\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}\\,(u-v)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}({\\int }_{-1}^{1}u-v\\,\\text{d}u)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}-vu]}_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}v \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(0-2v)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{[-{v}^{2}]}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414463 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b5899eb-77cf-5b34-b910-c93474d97e73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414432} {:answer/id #uuid "6d9b5bd3-6406-550a-9325-8fcec12f33ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414438} {:answer/id #uuid "8846a5c5-d563-5056-8e15-64f5e19db2bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414441} {:answer/id #uuid "8d5f84da-ca2b-5738-b498-90f08c5871a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414442} {:answer/id #uuid "f2e130f7-33b8-53a2-a94a-0675fb611102" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414454}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d9b5bd3-6406-550a-9325-8fcec12f33ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414438} :answer-field/id #uuid "831c917a-be0b-5705-bc09-e4bee9b35277" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414457}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f95319f6-2e43-511b-ae0d-9608a991c217" :question/math-academy-id "q-161179" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}(x+y+z)\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u+v,\\,3v,\\,2u-v⟩\n$$\n\n \nover the domain $D=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=3\\sqrt{6}.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=3\\sqrt{6}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(x+y+z)\\,{|}_{x=u+v\\,y=3v\\,z=2u-v} \\\\  & =(u+v)+(3v)+(2u-v) \\\\  & =3u+3v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(3u+3v)\\cdot 3\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =9\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{D}\\,(u+v)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =9\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({\\int }_{0}^{1}u+v\\,\\text{d}u)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =9\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}+vu]}_{0}^{1}\\text{d}v \\\\  & =9\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{2}+v)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =9\\sqrt{6}{[\\frac{v}{2}+\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =9\\sqrt{6}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =9\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418478 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10989bff-b98e-51af-bb4a-b28350a226e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418467} {:answer/id #uuid "1687e4a9-1caf-5ff9-a03a-04a61daf8637" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418468} {:answer/id #uuid "3d279b80-6077-585e-b090-d7cf3aa7efe0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418469} {:answer/id #uuid "60f276b3-36f3-5fbe-9d10-b5fec8b429a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418470} {:answer/id #uuid "b1865271-a8a9-5f44-b938-a958ba7e5f06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418472}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d279b80-6077-585e-b090-d7cf3aa7efe0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418469} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ec0f1fd-a976-5791-b23e-66968bb1089c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418476}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d41258e7-4727-5661-9adb-26ceb25d76d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-161185" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}(xy+xz)\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,2v,\\,6v⟩\n$$\n\n \nover the domain $D=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=2\\sqrt{10}.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=2\\sqrt{10}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(xy+xz)\\,{|}_{x=u\\,y=2v\\,z=6v} \\\\  & =(u)(2v)+(u)(6v) \\\\  & =8uv\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,8uv\\cdot 2\\sqrt{10}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =16\\sqrt{10}{∬}_{D}\\,uv\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =16\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}uv\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =16\\sqrt{10}({\\int }_{0}^{1}u\\,\\text{d}u)({\\int }_{0}^{2}v\\,\\text{d}v) \\\\  & =16\\sqrt{10}\\cdot {[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\cdot {[\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =16\\sqrt{10}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =16\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Surface Integrals Over Parametric Surfaces"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175987 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175838 :question/id #uuid "11d9077c-a0ac-5d42-9616-b8fe867d1db2" :question/math-academy-id "e-9076" :question/problem "![](images/ff/ffaf242238a8d32ce0f134cff6e0cf4256b2888b0a71f65e3c5ec470cbce284a.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above and the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \\left\\langle 2y + \\ln{(\\sqrt{x+5})}, \\: e^{y/3} + 3x \\right\\rangle.$ \nFind a repeated integral in plane polar coordinates that's equivalent to $\\displaystyle \\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r} \\, .$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}}\n=\n\\oint\\limits_{C} (2y + \\ln{(\\sqrt{x+5})}) \\: \\mathrm{d}x + \\left( e^{y/3} + 3x \\right) \\: \\mathrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y \n= \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/e4/e4c7913793a4b08916a832e575c5b2c167d5dfeec244eea23857731df1e514e7.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)= 2y + \\ln{(\\sqrt{x+5})}$ and $Q(x,y)=e^{y/3} + 3x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(2y+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{x+5}))\\,dx+({e}^{y/3}+3x)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3-2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 3, \\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e5704a2-acbe-5ccb-98dc-aa77e0ccd803" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176223 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6afcc73d-a8a6-5615-9d7c-fa0873dda7a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}2r\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242139} {:answer/id #uuid "87e2b68d-147e-555d-9b7c-88c63841a64f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}4r\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242371} {:answer/id #uuid "99c2aeae-ff81-519e-b9b7-d9f8716c6540" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}4r\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242523} {:answer/id #uuid "bc85f820-b7f6-5517-a066-986ad9b00f94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}({r}^{3}-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta })r\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242832} {:answer/id #uuid "f7a5a0d5-6ff6-5128-b57e-53fc5760b7d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}({r}^{3}-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta })\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243368}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87e2b68d-147e-555d-9b7c-88c63841a64f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}4r\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242371} :answer-field/id #uuid "488d900f-29ce-5ad3-9267-3a76fc467aa7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243575}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e6e3c388-6148-5c9d-8b7f-1f6ea60657b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-108544" :question/problem "![](images/d0/d0964ff8219d84181163537f5660fe2b504668b1e97e3461cc8cb43d18fc6091.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}(3y-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})\\,dx+(7x+\\sqrt{y+5})\\,dy\\,?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/de/deeb5648e328ecc93cf6c1de620e103da0f7677705d1e89e4654deff8d411f66.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=3y-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}$ and $Q(x,y)=7x+\\sqrt{y+5},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(3y-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})\\,dx+(7x+\\sqrt{y+5})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(7-3)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}4\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}4r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176313 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "378b0460-32af-5257-b39e-eb191e6b7ea0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-3{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )r\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257271} {:answer/id #uuid "45df6382-7cbc-520f-a94b-e0654045f8da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-3{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257393} {:answer/id #uuid "80f5da35-1f8d-5b8e-98bc-a87edfbce6e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}(r-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )r\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257928} {:answer/id #uuid "d9166bd6-4167-5918-9672-1af0c9421726" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}({r}^{2}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258683} {:answer/id #uuid "e9823d2d-a2cd-57c0-bf4a-ee850ecc8da8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}({r}^{2}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )r\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258810}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "378b0460-32af-5257-b39e-eb191e6b7ea0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-3{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )r\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257271} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca565c59-abfb-57f2-916c-26752c3de0b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259386}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "20585d4c-063f-50c6-8591-a785c62137ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-125719" :question/problem "![](images/8b/8ba69d7bfe8dd363288bbf86661172004b21143f02c70260da0c184f066a7c12.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y)=⟨{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{y}^{3},\\,y{e}^{y+1}+x⟩,$ which of the following is equivalent to ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr\\,?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{y}^{3})\\,dx+(y{e}^{y+1}+x)\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/3b/3b90abecc774b5ba5dcee19385fef38b14206d2c31c3b6cba38216bfa9957448.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{y}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=y{e}^{y+1}+x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{y}^{3})\\,dx-(y{e}^{y+1}+x)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(1-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(1-3\\cdot {r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-3{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262635 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f66aec3-ede9-595c-8bcb-7f54196da8ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{8}r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260432} {:answer/id #uuid "44e1870e-293c-5f0c-a3db-c07d702ba5c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{8}3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260475} {:answer/id #uuid "8aba7c6d-6a77-540a-a842-d5c4fb12827e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{8}3{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261085} {:answer/id #uuid "9147499e-717b-5f75-8693-2d766bf4b05c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{8}2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261136} {:answer/id #uuid "ce797455-43e3-53db-9124-847c7a894cd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{8}r(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261702}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8aba7c6d-6a77-540a-a842-d5c4fb12827e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{8}3{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261085} :answer-field/id #uuid "92cd0ab6-593b-5eba-98fd-1dae04fb3a26" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262383}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d49b23b5-65e4-52d8-8af9-8ed41cd5e7b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-126687" :question/problem "![](images/e4/e49bde14ba6097dfc7b153d5b93c2be2462b141a37357bfff826c852fc1136eb.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}(2-xy+2{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+xy)\\,dy\\,?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/8b/8b3c794f11c0a9be558e66c1c7a4ae4cd996037bbd6e491eefaa1a2e9000c596.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=2-xy+2{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}+xy,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-x+4y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x+y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(2-xy+2{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+xy)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+y-(-x+4y))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3x-3y)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 8,\\,-\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(3x-3y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{8}3{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262636 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04dd58ef-4b04-5352-bcdf-e5156b92820d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259917} {:answer/id #uuid "3509406d-87dd-5cbf-9357-f595d8330751" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260348} {:answer/id #uuid "78fa28b7-4b07-5bd5-ac2c-f175adfb8d58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}r(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260937} {:answer/id #uuid "ab43a82f-c1c3-5558-b7ba-045a5ed382c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261375} {:answer/id #uuid "c8ebf1d6-bf91-58cd-afe8-ad00f1045eef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261651}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab43a82f-c1c3-5558-b7ba-045a5ed382c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261375} :answer-field/id #uuid "05e1a501-c1de-55ec-8316-3866bf836e2f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262135}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bfc83011-612b-5b59-8833-f11d956fe6a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-126710" :question/problem "![](images/83/831b2131cf134ec21def0014a3121429ded4cd3afd98dfd1660b1e78c1a9ba93.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y)=⟨y(x+1),\\,x(y+1)⟩,$ which of the following is equivalent to ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr\\,?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}y(x+1)\\,dx+x(y+1)\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/d8/d8cd55f88d614bdb49aee6eaa0ff9d1d56f6f597cd892ee199a0130b53954b5b.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=y(x+1)$ and $Q(x,y)=x(y+1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=x+1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=y+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y(x+1)\\,dx+x(y+1)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y+1-(x+1))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y-x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that  \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(y-x)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262637 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "088226e5-e75c-5742-95e5-cf251dc2c044" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259954} {:answer/id #uuid "5fb6c965-52e9-544a-b893-b0aec4e0ed51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260702} {:answer/id #uuid "68106dca-1a59-5004-9754-bd47cf8b89f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260783} {:answer/id #uuid "d83492fb-fd44-5225-bf11-33dcf491c32d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261779} {:answer/id #uuid "dd74461d-0ba1-5d66-a17d-00ce3adbc2f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261832}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d83492fb-fd44-5225-bf11-33dcf491c32d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261779} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebd0d439-6074-5bac-8272-423170c14f7e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262538}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5b7ce0c4-9186-5034-8ec0-50be4181952e" :question/math-academy-id "q-126711" :question/problem "![](images/8c/8cb15d895d5baf46d359d811fce99b73238434eb01079d1199cfd99d3f52f222.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y)=⟨{y}^{2}+{x}^{2},\\,xy+y{e}^{y}⟩,$ which of the following is equivalent to ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr\\,?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}({y}^{2}+{x}^{2})\\,dx+(xy+y{e}^{y})\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/23/232ced663dcac27ce2775af66dd2ef215ed937e0f4eb5e3e6fb008a3d82dc069.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)={y}^{2}+{x}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=xy+y{e}^{y},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({y}^{2}+{x}^{2})\\,dx+(xy+y{e}^{y})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y-2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,-\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 0}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}-y\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362542 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96e5a331-6c2f-5b4c-be57-b591d54b128c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362544}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96e5a331-6c2f-5b4c-be57-b591d54b128c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362544} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff34a1ff-5130-50fc-a7fd-b6423f4c353f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362543} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b10e2db9-53ef-5db9-9a66-3579622f302d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362546}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b10e2db9-53ef-5db9-9a66-3579622f302d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362546} :answer-field/id #uuid "56fcd649-48f8-57d7-a858-5cc6c7ca0e40" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362545} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da39282e-1d0e-528e-9b68-9bb9823b0dee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362548}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da39282e-1d0e-528e-9b68-9bb9823b0dee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362548} :answer-field/id #uuid "859e7cbf-97d2-5fac-bf0f-330421ed46c7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362547}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8131655d-848c-5eb2-a4f8-11a5d73df386" :question/math-academy-id "q-169204" :question/problem "Write down the double integral from the previous part in polar coordinates.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${∬}_{D}-6xy\\,\\text{d}A\\,={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{a}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{b}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        where $a\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \nand $b\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-3}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}-6xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}-6(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}-3{r}^{3}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}-3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387685 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "359ca0ff-973b-506e-a465-b43c34cba969" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387687} {:answer/id #uuid "0ccab75f-a602-56ce-b1a3-cd10fa0dbc03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{3}\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387688} {:answer/id #uuid "c0dec30f-8c79-5a3a-acee-c51c3f82081c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{3}\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387689} {:answer/id #uuid "49ca9510-621f-51b6-90c6-5b9cbb3cdc98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387690} {:answer/id #uuid "9a2f6402-c9e4-529d-b588-36204d64955a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}(r+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ))\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387691}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ccab75f-a602-56ce-b1a3-cd10fa0dbc03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{3}\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387688} :answer-field/id #uuid "6bee84b5-4364-5ef7-b230-57a279a56f9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387686}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "21730a28-6e7f-5db6-af91-78ecf92baa4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-126514" :question/problem "![](images/c1/c1f8bb7ed82d345f8397ea9b01854f05f3f668acdcebf5d75ebbc5cb8de04fa6.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-{x}^{2}y)\\,dx+(x{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{y+1})\\,dy\\,?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/9e/9eceb8ab173995a8325fb1a8452baaa7f1071e569391e9a49b69c047d76f3b52.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-{x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)=x{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{y+1},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-{x}^{2}y)\\,dx+(x{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{y+1})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({y}^{2}-(-{x}^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{3}\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387699 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01b89c47-d0b2-5264-b994-b98209bb77be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{5}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387701} {:answer/id #uuid "2db9727e-440f-5931-aa1e-281498cdc499" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{5}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387702} {:answer/id #uuid "64f5a418-b56a-53fb-b2ff-23e7ac5231c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{5}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387703} {:answer/id #uuid "c8dbfa5f-5e35-5361-b706-416c1d369242" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{5}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387704} {:answer/id #uuid "fe4753f6-a11c-59ab-853f-472db759de2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{5}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387705}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe4753f6-a11c-59ab-853f-472db759de2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{5}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387705} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddb9d9aa-d773-5af6-b8b4-ba71799f08c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387700}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b97bdd87-b8e7-5fa0-84d3-dcb91e945069" :question/math-academy-id "q-126686" :question/problem "![](images/e4/e44543ad51864a7db6b8e80b5c560a1ac773a7537573014f737451af4d1209a7.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}(x{y}^{2}-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,dx+(2{x}^{2}y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,dy\\,?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/d8/d8ee02b2b7282349604dc6305c0b2be796cfdbd2937e06d089e660683a5785ad.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=x{y}^{2}-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $Q(x,y)=2{x}^{2}y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2xy-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4xy-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(x{y}^{2}-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,dx+(2{x}^{2}y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4xy-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-(2xy-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 5,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}2(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{5}2{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{5}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387706 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d925ce22-07b4-5c26-811c-95dafb568817" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{4}(2r-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387708} {:answer/id #uuid "5dd24fe2-ec3e-58f0-a4da-38274f190bdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{4}(2r{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387709} {:answer/id #uuid "7c628f7d-ee25-5920-bf22-2d9af94503cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{4}(r{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }-{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387710} {:answer/id #uuid "0acf37f8-41ac-5bc8-8f19-331dbeb6be67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{4}(2r-{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387711} {:answer/id #uuid "974e402e-ac40-50a9-b8e9-36fbed90d028" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{4}(2r-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387712}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0acf37f8-41ac-5bc8-8f19-331dbeb6be67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{4}(2r-{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387711} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5e912ff-c934-5812-954b-69ac3ae4ba0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387707}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a9090184-b791-5b13-8e38-15714ad9e0e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-126709" :question/problem "![](images/00/008f5dbaeb8c3c26008c49e97ed0490c444b049a991c2198399359a30b806b07.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y)=⟨{e}^{x/4}+x{y}^{2},\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}+2x⟩,$ which of the following is equivalent to ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr\\,?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}({e}^{x/4}+x{y}^{2})\\,dx+({e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}+2x)\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/c4/c40d9fdfd10b3be4153c9c8ea3a6302af3cf0392533767313c467bdf7dcec1d7.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)={e}^{x/4}+x{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}+2x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({e}^{x/4}+x{y}^{2})\\,dx+({e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}+2x)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2-2xy)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 0}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2-2xy)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(2-2\\cdot r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{4}(2r-{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416109 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c51a5c2-f91d-5c41-ab32-882df0c70ed8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{6}(-2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416077} {:answer/id #uuid "35da7698-acba-5a15-9327-eb06db3b24c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{6}(-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -r)\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416078} {:answer/id #uuid "4b5c6d03-4f8a-5ba0-9230-947fbebb29b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{6}({r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -r)\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416082} {:answer/id #uuid "89460c96-8227-5b1e-b670-b297b665d081" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{6}(-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416088} {:answer/id #uuid "a650e27c-da32-56b0-88b1-5dd26bdd90fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{6}(-2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -r)\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416095}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35da7698-acba-5a15-9327-eb06db3b24c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{6}(-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -r)\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416078} :answer-field/id #uuid "80955fcf-3708-5539-a929-c138089951ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416106}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "06de88f2-295c-546b-af7c-8eedcb3a386b" :question/math-academy-id "q-126708" :question/problem "![](images/e5/e54e2dc5c314ed1dda6ef9c60db5c5df3058ba120afec42bddbabc2622c3ae98.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y)=⟨x+y,\\,-xy⟩,$ which of the following is equivalent to ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr\\,?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(x+y)\\,dx+(-xy)\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/e1/e1c053e1cc4b4304c1302da2c57278200931b84e4ba687269d0ea89a99b523b8.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=x+y$ and $Q(x,y)=-xy,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(x+y)\\,dx+(-xy)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y-1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y-1)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 6,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(-y-1)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{6}(-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -r)\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416112 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48ad61f7-0826-5aab-ae4e-a6c43c772071" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416081} {:answer/id #uuid "5a99ee20-4856-5c51-8e4b-86d8dbf5a614" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416084} {:answer/id #uuid "a973df46-4a12-5e49-ab49-9f61bef424b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416097} {:answer/id #uuid "c06e1769-fccf-57f9-b3d1-36eb4847c17e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416101} {:answer/id #uuid "c3419ad6-a21f-5e70-a985-87306ae9808a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416102}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3419ad6-a21f-5e70-a985-87306ae9808a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416102} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4ef8539-57c5-5120-9995-c808fd171d2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416107}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fdb21538-b802-59ae-83c3-94940f8f1068" :question/math-academy-id "q-126516" :question/problem "![](images/bc/bc0660c37a6658635311a9a973ae3f4802740622a0b35ade22d6050ce648efa6.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}({y}^{3}-x)\\,dx+({x}^{3}+{e}^{1/y})\\,dy\\,?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/77/7724df0035511419dcb5117ba8aa337380325af22ee235d206a954aa12c62fe9.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)={y}^{3}-x$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{3}+{e}^{1/y},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({y}^{3}-x)\\,dx+({x}^{3}+{e}^{1/y})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}3({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}3({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Line Integral Along a Path as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176023 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175878 :question/id #uuid "1ee74b2f-adff-5c38-8b68-753b1c02f91c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9550" :question/problem "![](images/e1/e12a47b603d2c4193453d0f4daf18f54e52ed973498f520feef32cfafbe28a9f.png)\n\n  \n\nEvaluate ![](images/f1/f16fa431ecef10f5538168a49f4f3ceb4ec827114cab6854142fc9ca4d9ce9af.svg) where the path ![](images/c0/c0c348883f19c18a48444415da35aeaa27f8a18606bf5955e34aec416e248cad.svg) is shown above." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n![](images/ac/accf185e064f4c9c99cc6ad1437aabc7d5471cc332ff6c38d51a3f11ec6e26f2.svg)\n\nIn our case, the region ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) and its boundary ![](images/c0/c0c348883f19c18a48444415da35aeaa27f8a18606bf5955e34aec416e248cad.svg) satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem.\n\n![](images/67/6706cc6b412a98761f0f3cd29e584f1705fa8a708a926b805a8b5702a4f508e8.png)\n\nNow, since\n![](images/8c/8cbe1833f3c6f1612d69aecc8354e66cbf24ef426d648ed5c90224bbcf5ab341.svg)\nwe obtain\n\n![](images/01/01e3a66875647bfc9704bc6a1066dbc18c5cf1c0b0144aff8eb2e478685f83d2.svg)\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n![](images/49/493f02d8a665f5b2dedf5ab543475a28b82bc393d3290a60250d26fbb8065b01.svg)\n\nNow, notice that the region ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n![](images/2c/2c2141d090d129fde56fdfd334e4162927db247703b7ecd62eb14261340ac929.svg)\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n![](images/19/19dfe81d2ed118fd87e4e0ac483119e68f4bbf5ec67ccbaf2614360015ff193a.svg)\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n![](images/0c/0c7d035d915ae4a4cc993d476824897130b85797f32ad753f86e1accd1d5e29e.svg)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2eca638-7c7b-51ed-99e3-3b3156d13669" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176231 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10f2c80d-9cac-5756-9929-d92d4f8f3e17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416075} {:answer/id #uuid "168b8872-373b-550c-a253-e7487bbea803" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416076} {:answer/id #uuid "40901d5c-3eac-5579-8462-7e9d79976125" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416080} {:answer/id #uuid "6f76d14c-7e3b-59e6-b71d-5d52071de026" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416085} {:answer/id #uuid "7691659c-2eed-5772-8397-8d23f26bb55f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416087}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f76d14c-7e3b-59e6-b71d-5d52071de026" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416085} :answer-field/id #uuid "064abd0a-e4c9-5ad6-9b82-ef5bf4dea054" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416103}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ac600244-dbff-596b-9fbb-094ec0801e81" :question/math-academy-id "q-109119" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$ where $F(x,y)=⟨{x}^{2}y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,4-x{y}^{2}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ oriented counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}({x}^{2}y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,dx+(4-x{y}^{2})\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/09/09a69771617d73af6e4a1a42720223951e2e6edeb71a8df4d12aeb1bc22031aa.png)\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y)={x}^{2}y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,Q(x,y)=4-x{y}^{2},\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({x}^{2}y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,dx+(4-x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-{y}^{2}-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}-({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-4[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-4(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =-8\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176312 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a53b9ab-8a72-5248-8014-28838f92a12e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174131} {:answer/id #uuid "a37fc1b4-8a1e-514c-83d4-00d02b79fc44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174132} {:answer/id #uuid "ea2405ee-c89c-513f-a683-9070c1855719" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174133} {:answer/id #uuid "2e7b7361-c4e7-59c3-b526-8e2029ccdf7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174134} {:answer/id #uuid "33ddd78d-6745-50b2-a656-f83ea4f3b750" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174135}] :answer-field/id #uuid "86f67038-add1-5bf7-87fe-fead30bf61e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174130}] :question/id #uuid "552ffe88-dfcd-57ba-97f8-f7bf0a708429" :question/math-academy-id "q-125717" :question/problem "![](images/66/66ab1a3e2e7244a9b3e5fc9bda814736194c28dd9438fbb97086e99be4633261.png)\n\nEvaluate $∮_(C)(\\sqrt{x} - 4x^{2}y)dx + (e^{y} + 4xy^{2})dy$, where the path $C$ is shown above."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262640 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06945f99-44ac-5640-b54f-bce0077123f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259935} {:answer/id #uuid "bb1ac15a-5785-5358-9f30-8bef0b1aff07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{125\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261522} {:answer/id #uuid "d37903c6-09d2-5a3f-bf75-c74f7c5fbdec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261735} {:answer/id #uuid "e512847b-77b3-5cd2-87d1-d23710510d68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "625\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261890} {:answer/id #uuid "ff79ed24-25be-5e20-bded-431d31365687" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{625\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262115}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff79ed24-25be-5e20-bded-431d31365687" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{625\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262115} :answer-field/id #uuid "889bd836-2a14-565c-95a2-9c72a89ea2ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262362}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4a75ae5f-913c-5dd5-a1ba-18a248f691d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-126733" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$ where $F(x,y)=⟨2-{x}^{2}y,1+x{y}^{2}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25,$ oriented counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(2-{x}^{2}y)\\,dx+(1+x{y}^{2})\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/d0/d081f4dc19484f0c483c5518295c7c3b7195bb096404fb7ff6e27d8bda1cccc1.png)\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y)=2-{x}^{2}y,\\,Q(x,y)=1+x{y}^{2},\n$$\n\n we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(2-{x}^{2}y)\\,dx+(1+x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({y}^{2}-(-{x}^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({y}^{2}+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 5,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{5}{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{5}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{5}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{625}{4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{625}{4}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{625}{4}(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =\\frac{625\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262641 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "354b2453-2340-5b1a-b035-13af6216e390" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260350} {:answer/id #uuid "678d8b1a-b4fb-5ecd-9b33-15222113a168" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260779} {:answer/id #uuid "a144f162-db84-521f-aac9-d31fa7f5c9de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261277} {:answer/id #uuid "b4d7ee8d-a24f-5376-9f95-9be5fc03201d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261467} {:answer/id #uuid "f3e469d8-39d1-5c40-aeb7-1753437104ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262006}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3e469d8-39d1-5c40-aeb7-1753437104ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262006} :answer-field/id #uuid "46d81e6d-01cf-501c-b61e-4c6039a60547" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262247}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "388e817a-4cb8-54d8-b3b7-fede3e086b1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-126736" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$ where $F(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2{x}^{2}y,\\text{ }\\,\\sqrt{y+3}+2x{y}^{2}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ oriented counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2{x}^{2}y)\\,dx+(\\sqrt{y+3}-2x{y}^{2})\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/90/902790a00a049bbfc759403125ad5f19a8c2528a46b59c721ac43fb137cd5d8e.png)\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2{x}^{2}y,\\,Q(x,y)=\\sqrt{y+3}+2x{y}^{2},\n$$\n\n we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2{x}^{2}y)\\,dx+(\\sqrt{y+3}-2x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2{y}^{2}-(-2{x}^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}2({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262647 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "261c0683-b5fd-5378-9f79-2f86d8986e15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260208} {:answer/id #uuid "662bd605-3ab9-5488-a002-c8fa16319af2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260763} {:answer/id #uuid "8b57ae42-c1de-5f2a-b039-be8db7894670" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261090} {:answer/id #uuid "97f70e2c-3eb9-57dc-b585-0a203ce8b1b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261193} {:answer/id #uuid "f5992f2b-a16d-5638-b86c-c0c08b116db2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262024}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "662bd605-3ab9-5488-a002-c8fa16319af2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260763} :answer-field/id #uuid "de116324-372b-5aa0-acac-810021212cc8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262508}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ec2130ce-f120-59d6-bb98-ec9c31641d08" :question/math-academy-id "q-126747" :question/problem "![](images/21/21e8aa9acdca87acf153b2e7ad3f5e51d5708f53b81562e682e16098721d752a.png)\n\nEvaluate ${∮}_{C}({3}^{x}+{x}^{2}y)dx+({2}^{y}+x{y}^{2})\\,dy,$ where the path $C$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/92/922ae907661e21ea5510dc0d02f151afc4f4423ad11a572ede6849c623397baa.png)\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y)={3}^{x}+{x}^{2}y,\\,Q(x,y)={2}^{y}+x{y}^{2},\n$$\n\n we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({3}^{x}+{x}^{2}y)dx+({2}^{y}+x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}({r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{\\int }_{0}^{4}-{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ){\\int }_{0}^{4}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ){[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-64{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-64{[\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{2}]}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-32[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0] \\\\  & =-16\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262648 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0683defb-b18a-5104-ba2b-2d172de9437a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259934} {:answer/id #uuid "1a07b2c9-5a32-5442-aa95-42c397e429db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260098} {:answer/id #uuid "2551ce74-532c-5f28-af57-3ffd6ddde697" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260200} {:answer/id #uuid "71f89f57-5c33-5f0e-9d29-6a394cf2c9e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260871} {:answer/id #uuid "fd24ebdc-0496-5e63-96aa-2f262b5a9c20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262093}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2551ce74-532c-5f28-af57-3ffd6ddde697" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260200} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e227f31-00b9-570c-bcee-be2f54e3ed07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262405}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c830bb6a-6822-5681-99a1-ffb4d98dc8d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-126755" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$ where $F(x,y)=⟨{4}^{x}-{y}^{3}+2,\\,\\frac{2}{{y}^{2}+1}+{x}^{3}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2,$ oriented counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}({4}^{x}-{y}^{3}+2)\\,dx+(\\frac{2}{{y}^{2}+1}+{x}^{3})\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/c8/c8ebdae5c3fb2c2ca7b318f32a357589d73bbe0d6471877cbf8babd813edd395.png)\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y)={4}^{x}-{y}^{3}+2,\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{2}{{y}^{2}+1}+{x}^{3},\n$$\n\n we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({4}^{x}-{y}^{3}+2)\\,dx+(\\frac{2}{{y}^{2}+1}+{x}^{3})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3{x}^{2}-(-3{y}^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}3({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}3({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =3(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =6\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262649 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12a22b31-2af0-5b06-9a83-6c218c2bccec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260038} {:answer/id #uuid "268d0b01-2071-5ce9-af67-d8d07bc7d498" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{81\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260213} {:answer/id #uuid "27fcc03b-e7ec-5cd6-8c17-d9ed415fbdd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260226} {:answer/id #uuid "8d8ff48a-760c-518f-a7a3-eb1811f9959f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261115} {:answer/id #uuid "a6aa5623-cd60-5cc3-8f6a-687f5cc6e34b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261328}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "268d0b01-2071-5ce9-af67-d8d07bc7d498" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{81\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260213} :answer-field/id #uuid "43c9c6b7-94d1-5f0d-8201-97dc531b22fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262240}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c0c6144e-7780-5bef-857b-0b92844c7ba9" :question/math-academy-id "q-126756" :question/problem "![](images/d6/d675e8d06f2037531a2eec3a7e48374bb0d2e5cb59adfee0cbee5ce3a23add67.png)\n\nEvaluate ${∮}_{C}({e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}y)dx+({e}^{y}+2x{y}^{2})\\,dy,$ where the path $C$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/96/96998e4a17d20459622486970518a677309b23fd936c993d8c97dcf0d65a9a29.png)\n\n \n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y)={e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}y,\\,Q(x,y)={e}^{y}+2x{y}^{2},\n$$\n\n we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}y)dx+({e}^{y}+2x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2{y}^{2}-(-2{x}^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2({y}^{2}+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2({y}^{2}+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}2({r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}2{r}^{3}({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}2{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{2}]}_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\frac{81}{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}[\\theta ]_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{81\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262650 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "150e4cdf-338e-59c7-bd21-7afe84d888b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260059} {:answer/id #uuid "36abd901-c8ba-5d38-a5c9-52c30378dadf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260363} {:answer/id #uuid "9d666cad-5bfd-565d-8739-349a89a1addf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{90\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261238} {:answer/id #uuid "a5bca1ec-b5b5-53a5-8432-35d6656bef12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{45\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261314} {:answer/id #uuid "ea5fdf8c-c1cf-53bf-9f78-dc3ffff862be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261931}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "150e4cdf-338e-59c7-bd21-7afe84d888b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260059} :answer-field/id #uuid "abcd46d2-644b-54bb-992b-feba4db98108" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262433}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5041cff1-c6ce-564b-b2c4-39a44d615f75" :question/math-academy-id "q-126758" :question/problem "![](images/bc/bcdc0f59fa284c2b1f1621e7de057ef6f0e084d2eca6732fbc5eae3f74ede113.png)\n\nEvaluate ${∮}_{C}(\\sqrt{5+x}-3{x}^{2}y)dx+({e}^{-{y}^{2}}+3x{y}^{2})\\,dy,$ where the path $C$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/62/6273034b38059d3d49b2a29c91052c290151b4a29073f1c925172dd1c4c2bf72.png)\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\sqrt{5+x}-3{x}^{2}y,\\,Q(x,y)={e}^{-{y}^{2}}+3x{y}^{2},\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-3{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(\\sqrt{5+x}-3{x}^{2}y)dx+({e}^{-{y}^{2}}+x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3{y}^{2}-(-3{x}^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,2\\le r\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}3({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}3({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{2}^{4}3{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{2}^{4}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{2}^{4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}60\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =180[\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =180\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =90\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262651 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ed58d08-ecaa-599c-9195-c29b35269e11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260015} {:answer/id #uuid "93aa6614-b6f1-5782-8504-d218d250802f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261157} {:answer/id #uuid "94458430-8e1f-5e26-b6c8-6071eb5169db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261166} {:answer/id #uuid "ba4d8f66-99a1-572a-81e7-43c27f0e448c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261511} {:answer/id #uuid "dcac3080-ad1d-58a2-89d2-fd5a233d7141" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261825}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93aa6614-b6f1-5782-8504-d218d250802f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261157} :answer-field/id #uuid "2887dfa3-45a9-5407-8ca4-04a9ffa5e4e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262198}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "546a427c-f103-5c76-afb8-9ff327ad2cbf" :question/math-academy-id "q-126759" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$ where $F(x,y)=⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{x+5}-{y}^{3}}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{y+5}+{x}^{3}}{3}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ oriented counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+5}-{y}^{3}}{3}\\,dx+\\frac{\\sqrt{y+5}+{x}^{3}}{3}\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/09/09a69771617d73af6e4a1a42720223951e2e6edeb71a8df4d12aeb1bc22031aa.png)\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\frac{\\sqrt{x+5}-{y}^{3}}{3},\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{\\sqrt{y+5}+{x}^{3}}{3},\n$$\n\n we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+5}-{y}^{3}}{3}\\,dx+\\frac{\\sqrt{y+5}+{x}^{3}}{3}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-(-{y}^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =4(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =8\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416110 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cf2dceb-7c5d-5d9b-9403-414a88730866" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\text{ }\\,\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416074} {:answer/id #uuid "4f5d7b5b-5c88-5245-85a2-eb913d758a87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{729\\text{ }\\,\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416083} {:answer/id #uuid "96c867f3-7baa-5a33-8eba-e465e7463140" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{81\\text{ }\\,\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416090} {:answer/id #uuid "990e60bc-7dbd-5a34-81df-2112a3293326" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{243\\text{ }\\,\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416092} {:answer/id #uuid "abee63b5-df6a-5f86-838c-404f4f8edb05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27\\text{ }\\,\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416098}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "990e60bc-7dbd-5a34-81df-2112a3293326" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{243\\text{ }\\,\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416092} :answer-field/id #uuid "07315ac9-2ead-596d-85c1-f7d066447650" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416104}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "654eb25e-4dd2-5095-910d-a25787a1e455" :question/math-academy-id "q-126753" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$ where $F(x,y)=⟨{sin}^{3}⁡x-{y}^{3},{y}^{4}+{x}^{3}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9,$ oriented counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}({sin}^{3}⁡x-{y}^{3})\\,dx+({y}^{4}+{x}^{3})\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/e2/e218f4b6938b166aa6eacab02ce0276a4a863a0cfade8528816a54c2621b51e7.png)\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y)={sin}^{3}⁡x-{y}^{3},\\,Q(x,y)={y}^{4}+{x}^{3},\n$$\n\n we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({sin}^{3}⁡x-{y}^{3})\\,dx+({y}^{4}+{x}^{3})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3{x}^{2}-(-3{y}^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}3({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}3({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{81}{4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{243}{4}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{243}{4}(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =\\frac{243\\text{ }\\,\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Line Integral Using Green's Theorem"}]}
